(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("sre",{"sre/slos-and-error-budgets":{"html":"\u003cp>&quot;The site should be reliable&quot; is not a target. Nobody can tell whether it was met, whether it is worth more investment, or when to stop shipping features and fix things. Site reliability engineering gives reliability a number, an agreed target, and a rule for what to do when the target is at risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>SLIs, SLOs and SLAs, and how they differ\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing good indicators\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Calculating an error budget\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the budget to guide decisions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-terms\">Three terms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SLI\u003c/strong> (indicator)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A measurement of service behaviour\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Share of requests that succeed and finish within 300 ms\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SLO\u003c/strong> (objective)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An internal target for an SLI over a window\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9% of requests are good over 30 days\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SLA\u003c/strong> (agreement)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A contract with consequences, usually financial\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Credits if monthly availability drops below 99.5%\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The SLO is stricter than the SLA, so you notice trouble before you owe a customer anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-slis\">Choosing SLIs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure what users experience, from as close to the user as practical (load balancer or client, not an internal health check). A good SLI is a \u003cstrong>ratio of good events to valid events\u003c/strong>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SLI = good requests / valid requests\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">SLI = good requests / valid requests\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Service type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Useful SLIs\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Request-driven API\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Availability (non-5xx ratio), latency (share faster than a threshold)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Data pipeline\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Freshness (data newer than N minutes), correctness, completeness\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Durability, availability\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Batch job\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Completed on schedule\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pick a few per service. Three to five is plenty, and focus on the \u003cstrong>critical user journeys\u003c/strong>, such as sign in, search and checkout, rather than every endpoint.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"setting-the-target\">Setting the target\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Higher is not always better. Each extra nine costs far more and slows delivery. Users&#39; own devices and networks fail too, so past a point they cannot tell the difference.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Availability target\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Downtime allowed per 30 days\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>99%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 7 hours 12 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>99.9%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 43 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>99.95%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 21 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>99.99%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 4 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Calculate it yourself: a 30-day window has 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is 43.2 minutes. Start from what users actually need, look at what you currently achieve, and set a target you can meet with reasonable effort, then tighten it deliberately.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-error-budget\">The error budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>error budget\u003c/strong> is the amount of unreliability the SLO allows:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"error budget = 1 - SLO\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">error budget = 1 - SLO\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For a 99.9% SLO over 30 days, you may fail 0.1% of requests. If you serve 10 million requests, that is 10,000 failures. The budget turns reliability into something you can spend:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Budget remaining\u003c/strong>: ship features, run experiments, make risky changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Budget low or exhausted\u003c/strong>: slow down. Prioritize reliability work, freeze risky releases, and fix what is burning the budget.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>This is the heart of the idea: product and engineering share one number, so the argument &quot;features versus stability&quot; becomes a decision rule agreed in advance. Reliability is a feature that competes for the same effort.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-it\">Measuring it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Availability SLI over 30 days\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{service=&quot;shop-api&quot;, status!~&quot;5..&quot;}[30d]))\n  /\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{service=&quot;shop-api&quot;}[30d]))\n\n# Fraction of the error budget remaining (99.9% SLO)\n1 - (\n  (1 - (sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=&quot;shop-api&quot;, status!~&quot;5..&quot;}[30d]))\n        / sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=&quot;shop-api&quot;}[30d]))))\n  / 0.001\n)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Availability SLI over 30 days\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">status\u003c/span>!~\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;5..&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>d]))\n  /\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>d]))\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Fraction of the error budget remaining (99.9% SLO)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> - (\n  (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> - (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">status\u003c/span>!~\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;5..&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>d]))\n        / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>d]))))\n  / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.001\u003c/span>\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Long-range queries like these are costly, so teams precompute them with recording rules. Display the remaining budget on the service dashboard where everyone can see it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How SLIs, SLOs and error budgets fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Three terms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Choosing SLIs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Setting the target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The error budget\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measuring it\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Three terms to Choosing SLIs; 2, Choosing SLIs to Setting the target; 3, Setting the target to The error budget; 4, The error budget to Measuring it.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Three terms \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The SLO is stricter than the SLA, so you notice trouble before you owe a customer anything.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Setting SLOs at 100% or at whatever the service happens to do today.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Choosing SLIs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Measure what users experience, from as close to the user as practical (load balancer or client, not an internal health check).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Measuring server health instead of user experience.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Setting the target \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Higher is not always better. Each extra nine costs far more and slows delivery.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> SLOs with no policy for what happens when the budget is spent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>The error budget \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The error budget is the amount of unreliability the SLO allows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many SLOs, none of them used to make decisions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Measuring it \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Long-range queries like these are costly, so teams precompute them with recording rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-budget-is-gone-on-day-twelve\">Incident: the budget is gone on day twelve\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The checkout SLO is 99.9% over 30 days. On day 12 the dashboard shows the budget at -20%: more failures have occurred than the whole month allows.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The numbers say what must happen next, and the policy should already have said so. Look at what consumed it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Budget spent (12 days):\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Release 3.2.0 rollback (day 4):      55%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Payment provider timeouts (day 8):   40%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Everything else:                     25%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two events made up 95% of the loss. The agreed response is to pause non-urgent feature releases for the service, spend the sprint on the causes (a safer rollout with automatic canary abort, and timeout and fallback behaviour for the payment provider), and review again when the budget recovers as the window moves on. Importantly, the conversation was about data and a policy everyone had signed up to, not about whose fault it was. If the budget is \u003cem>never\u003c/em> used, that is also a signal: the target may be too strict, and you could be shipping faster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-budget\">Predict the budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An SLO is 99.9 percent over 30 days. About how many minutes of downtime does the error budget allow?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> About 43 minutes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>30 days is 43,200 minutes. One tenth of one percent of that is 43.2 minutes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service has used 80 percent of its budget in the first week. What is a sensible response?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Slow risky releases and fix reliability work first.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The budget is a tool for deciding. High burn early means the rest of the period is at risk.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting SLOs at 100% or at whatever the service happens to do today\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring server health instead of user experience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Too many SLOs, none of them used to make decisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SLOs with no policy for what happens when the budget is spent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-terms","text":"Three terms"},{"id":"choosing-slis","text":"Choosing SLIs"},{"id":"setting-the-target","text":"Setting the target"},{"id":"the-error-budget","text":"The error budget"},{"id":"measuring-it","text":"Measuring it"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-budget-is-gone-on-day-twelve","text":"Incident: the budget is gone on day twelve"},{"id":"predict-the-budget","text":"Predict the budget"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: service level objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"},{"t":"SRE workbook: error budget policy","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/"},{"t":"SRE workbook: alerting on SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"}],"text":"\"The site should be reliable\" is not a target. Nobody can tell whether it was met, whether it is worth more investment, or when to stop shipping features and fix things. Site reliability engineering gives reliability a number, an agreed target, and a rule for what to do when the target is at risk. What you will learn SLIs, SLOs and SLAs, and how they differ Choosing good indicators Calculating an error budget Using the budget to guide decisions Three terms Term Meaning Example SLI (indicator) A measurement of service behaviour Share of requests that succeed and finish within 300 ms SLO (objective) An internal target for an SLI over a window 99.9% of requests are good over 30 days SLA (agreement) A contract with consequences, usually financial Credits if monthly availability drops below 99.5% The SLO is stricter than the SLA, so you notice trouble before you owe a customer anything. Choosing SLIs Measure what users experience, from as close to the user as practical (load balancer or client, not an internal health check). A good SLI is a ratio of good events to valid events : SLI = good requests / valid requests Service type Useful SLIs Request-driven API Availability (non-5xx ratio), latency (share faster than a threshold) Data pipeline Freshness (data newer than N minutes), correctness, completeness Storage Durability, availability Batch job Completed on schedule Pick a few per service. Three to five is plenty, and focus on the critical user journeys , such as sign in, search and checkout, rather than every endpoint. Setting the target Higher is not always better. Each extra nine costs far more and slows delivery. Users&#39; own devices and networks fail too, so past a point they cannot tell the difference. Availability target Downtime allowed per 30 days 99% about 7 hours 12 minutes 99.9% about 43 minutes 99.95% about 21 minutes 99.99% about 4 minutes Calculate it yourself: a 30-day window has 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is 43.2 minutes. Start from what users actually need, look at what you currently achieve, and set a target you can meet with reasonable effort, then tighten it deliberately. The error budget The error budget is the amount of unreliability the SLO allows: error budget = 1 - SLO For a 99.9% SLO over 30 days, you may fail 0.1% of requests. If you serve 10 million requests, that is 10,000 failures. The budget turns reliability into something you can spend: Budget remaining : ship features, run experiments, make risky changes. Budget low or exhausted : slow down. Prioritize reliability work, freeze risky releases, and fix what is burning the budget. This is the heart of the idea: product and engineering share one number, so the argument \"features versus stability\" becomes a decision rule agreed in advance. Reliability is a feature that competes for the same effort. Measuring it # Availability SLI over 30 days sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ service = \"shop-api\" , status !~ \"5..\" }[ 30 d])) / sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ service = \"shop-api\" }[ 30 d])) # Fraction of the error budget remaining (99.9% SLO) 1 - ( ( 1 - ( sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ service = \"shop-api\" , status !~ \"5..\" }[ 30 d])) / sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ service = \"shop-api\" }[ 30 d])))) / 0.001 ) Long-range queries like these are costly, so teams precompute them with recording rules. Display the remaining budget on the service dashboard where everyone can see it. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How SLIs, SLOs and error budgets fits together Flow in order: 1, Three terms to Choosing SLIs; 2, Choosing SLIs to Setting the target; 3, Setting the target to The error budget; 4, The error budget to Measuring it. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Three terms Step What it does: The SLO is st"},"sre/incident-response":{"html":"\u003cp>At 03:12 an alert fires. The next hour decides how much a problem costs. Teams that handle incidents well are not smarter than others. They rehearsed a process so nobody has to invent one under stress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Incident roles and who does what\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Severity levels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The sequence: detect, triage, mitigate, resolve\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Communication during an incident\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On anything beyond a trivial incident, split the work so one person is not debugging, deciding and answering questions at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Responsibility\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Incident commander (IC)\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs the response, makes decisions, assigns work. Does \u003cem>not\u003c/em> debug\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Operations lead\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Investigates and applies changes to the systems\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Communications lead\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Posts updates to stakeholders and customers on a fixed rhythm\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Scribe\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Records a timeline of what was seen and done, with timestamps\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For small incidents one person may hold several roles, but the IC role should still be explicit: &quot;I am IC.&quot; If the IC begins digging into the problem, they should hand command to someone else first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"severity\">Severity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define levels in advance so nobody argues during the incident. An example scheme:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Impact\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Response\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SEV1\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Major outage or data loss; most users affected\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Page everyone needed, all hands, updates every 15-30 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SEV2\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Significant degradation; some users or a key feature affected\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Page on-call, updates every 30-60 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SEV3\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minor impact; workaround exists\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Handle in working hours\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When in doubt, declare the higher level. It is cheap to downgrade and expensive to escalate late.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-sequence\">The sequence\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect.\u003c/strong> An alert, a customer report or a colleague. Acknowledge it so others know someone owns it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Triage.\u003c/strong> How bad is it, who is affected, is it getting worse? Assign severity and open a dedicated channel.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Mitigate.\u003c/strong> \u003cstrong>Stop the harm first.\u003c/strong> Roll back, fail over, disable a feature flag, shift traffic, scale up. Do not wait for the root cause.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Diagnose.\u003c/strong> Once users are no longer hurt, find out why, working from evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Resolve.\u003c/strong> Confirm the metrics recovered and stayed recovered.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Follow up.\u003c/strong> Schedule the postmortem and track the actions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ask early: &quot;What changed?&quot; Most incidents trace back to a recent deployment, configuration change, dependency, or traffic pattern. Checking the deployment log and change calendar first finds many causes in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Here is what that first check looks like in practice on a Kubernetes service:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop | tail -3\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -8\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.deployment\\.kubernetes\\.io/revision}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.metadata.annotations.deployment\\.kubernetes\\.io/revision}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the newest revision was rolled out minutes before the alert fired, that is your first suspect, and \u003ccode>kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/code> is the mitigation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Prefer mitigations that are \u003cstrong>reversible, well understood and fast\u003c/strong>. A rollback to a known good version beats a clever hot fix written at 3 a.m.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"communication\">Communication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use \u003cstrong>one channel\u003c/strong> for the incident and keep debate out of the status updates.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Post updates on a \u003cstrong>schedule\u003c/strong>, even when the update is &quot;no change, still investigating, next update at 03:45&quot;.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>State \u003cstrong>impact, status and next step\u003c/strong>: facts, not guesses.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep a running \u003cstrong>timeline\u003c/strong> with timestamps for the postmortem.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A good update:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"03:30 UTC - SEV2 - Checkout errors\nImpact: about 15% of checkout attempts fail with an error page.\nStatus: Cause identified as release 3.2.0. Rollback in progress.\nNext update: 03:45 UTC or sooner if status changes.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">03:30 UTC - SEV2 - Checkout errors\nImpact: about 15% of checkout attempts fail with an error page.\nStatus: Cause identified as release 3.2.0. Rollback in progress.\nNext update: 03:45 UTC or sooner if status changes.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handover-and-fatigue\">Handover and fatigue\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Long incidents need fresh people. Hand over explicitly with a summary: what is broken, what has been tried, current theory, who is doing what. Rotate the IC after a few hours, and make sure people eat and rest. Tired people make mistakes that turn a bad night into a worse one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-people-fix-the-same-thing-differently\">Incident: two people fix the same thing differently\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Checkout is failing. One engineer rolls back the release. Another, unaware, scales the database up. A third restarts the API Pods. Errors continue, and nobody can tell which action helped or hurt.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Uncoordinated changes destroy your ability to learn what worked and can make things worse. The first action of a good IC is to establish control:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"IC: I'm taking command of this incident. Freeze all changes unless I approve them.\nIC: Ops lead is Dana. Dana, you make changes to production. Everyone else reports findings to the scribe.\nIC: Hypothesis: release 3.2.0. Dana, roll back now. We will watch error rate for 5 minutes.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">IC: I&#x27;m taking command of this incident. Freeze all changes unless I approve them.\nIC: Ops lead is Dana. Dana, you make changes to production. Everyone else reports findings to the scribe.\nIC: Hypothesis: release 3.2.0. Dana, roll back now. We will watch error rate for 5 minutes.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One change at a time, announced before it is made, with a metric to watch afterwards. The rollback restores service within four minutes, and because only one change was made, the cause is clear. The other changes (the database scale-up) are queued for review rather than left in place without understanding.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"det&gt;tri;tri&gt;mit;tri&gt;com;mit&gt;rev;com&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The shape of an incident\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"det\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Detect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tri\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Triage and declare\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mit\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mitigate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"com\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Communicate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Detect to Triage and declare; 2, Triage and declare to Mitigate; 3, Triage and declare to Communicate; 4, Mitigate to Review; 5, Communicate to Review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"det\">\u003cdt>Detect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An alert or a customer report starts the incident.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow detection means longer impact.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tri\">\u003cdt>Triage and declare \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Set severity, name an incident lead and open a channel.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a lead everyone investigates and nobody decides.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mit\">\u003cdt>Mitigate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Restore service first: rollback, failover or disable a feature.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Digging for root cause first prolongs the outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"com\">\u003cdt>Communicate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Regular updates to stakeholders and a status page.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silence creates extra interruptions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A blameless review with action items and owners.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without follow-up the same incident returns.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> The site is down, so every engineer investigates on their own.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># #general\n&quot;I am looking at the database&quot;\n&quot;I am restarting the cache&quot;\n&quot;I deployed a fix&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>Two people restarted the cache twice. The deploy overlapped the restart. The outage grew from 10 to 50 minutes.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Without a single coordinator, people duplicate work, make conflicting changes and nobody knows the current state. Change itself is a common cause of making incidents worse.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># declare an incident\nIncident commander: Asha\nOps lead: Ben (runs changes)\nComms: Chen (updates every 15 min)\n# one channel, one timeline, one change at a time\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Name an incident commander, split roles, make one change at a time and write down what was done.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Debugging before mitigating\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Several people changing production at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Silence: no updates for stakeholders\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No clear incident commander\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a long incident without handover or rest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"severity","text":"Severity"},{"id":"the-sequence","text":"The sequence"},{"id":"communication","text":"Communication"},{"id":"handover-and-fatigue","text":"Handover and fatigue"},{"id":"incident-two-people-fix-the-same-thing-differently","text":"Incident: two people fix the same thing differently"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: managing incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"},{"t":"PagerDuty incident response documentation","u":"https://response.pagerduty.com/"},{"t":"SRE book: practical alerting","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/practical-alerting/"}],"text":"At 03:12 an alert fires. The next hour decides how much a problem costs. Teams that handle incidents well are not smarter than others. They rehearsed a process so nobody has to invent one under stress. What you will learn Incident roles and who does what Severity levels The sequence: detect, triage, mitigate, resolve Communication during an incident Roles On anything beyond a trivial incident, split the work so one person is not debugging, deciding and answering questions at once. Role Responsibility Incident commander (IC) Runs the response, makes decisions, assigns work. Does not debug Operations lead Investigates and applies changes to the systems Communications lead Posts updates to stakeholders and customers on a fixed rhythm Scribe Records a timeline of what was seen and done, with timestamps For small incidents one person may hold several roles, but the IC role should still be explicit: \"I am IC.\" If the IC begins digging into the problem, they should hand command to someone else first. Severity Define levels in advance so nobody argues during the incident. An example scheme: Level Impact Response SEV1 Major outage or data loss; most users affected Page everyone needed, all hands, updates every 15-30 minutes SEV2 Significant degradation; some users or a key feature affected Page on-call, updates every 30-60 minutes SEV3 Minor impact; workaround exists Handle in working hours When in doubt, declare the higher level. It is cheap to downgrade and expensive to escalate late. The sequence Detect. An alert, a customer report or a colleague. Acknowledge it so others know someone owns it. Triage. How bad is it, who is affected, is it getting worse? Assign severity and open a dedicated channel. Mitigate. Stop the harm first. Roll back, fail over, disable a feature flag, shift traffic, scale up. Do not wait for the root cause. Diagnose. Once users are no longer hurt, find out why, working from evidence. Resolve. Confirm the metrics recovered and stayed recovered. Follow up. Schedule the postmortem and track the actions. Tip Ask early: \"What changed?\" Most incidents trace back to a recent deployment, configuration change, dependency, or traffic pattern. Checking the deployment log and change calendar first finds many causes in minutes. Here is what that first check looks like in practice on a Kubernetes service: $ kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop | tail -3 $ kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -8 $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.metadata.annotations.deployment\\.kubernetes\\.io/revision}&#x27; If the newest revision was rolled out minutes before the alert fired, that is your first suspect, and kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop is the mitigation. Prefer mitigations that are reversible, well understood and fast . A rollback to a known good version beats a clever hot fix written at 3 a.m. Communication Use one channel for the incident and keep debate out of the status updates. Post updates on a schedule , even when the update is \"no change, still investigating, next update at 03:45\". State impact, status and next step : facts, not guesses. Keep a running timeline with timestamps for the postmortem. A good update: 03:30 UTC - SEV2 - Checkout errors Impact: about 15% of checkout attempts fail with an error page. Status: Cause identified as release 3.2.0. Rollback in progress. Next update: 03:45 UTC or sooner if status changes. Handover and fatigue Long incidents need fresh people. Hand over explicitly with a summary: what is broken, what has been tried, current theory, who is doing what. Rotate the IC after a few hours, and make sure people eat and rest. Tired people make mistakes that turn a bad night into a worse one. Incident: two people fix the same thing differently Incident Checkout is failing. One engineer rolls back the release. Another, unaware, scales the database up. A third restarts the API Pods. Errors continue, and nobody can tell which actio"},"sre/postmortems-and-toil":{"html":"\u003cp>An incident that teaches nothing will repeat. The postmortem turns a bad night into lasting improvement, but only if people feel safe describing what actually happened. And between incidents, the daily drudgery called toil quietly consumes the time you would need to prevent the next one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What blameless means and why it matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The structure of a useful postmortem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing action items that get done\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining and reducing toil\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blameless-does-not-mean-accountability-free\">Blameless does not mean accountability-free\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Complex systems fail because of many contributing factors, not a single careless person. If people are punished for mistakes, they hide them, and the organization loses the knowledge it needs. \u003cstrong>Blameless\u003c/strong> means we ask &quot;how did our systems, processes and tooling make this mistake easy and its impact large?&quot; rather than &quot;who did it?&quot;\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-why-it-matters\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Why it matters\u003c/div>\u003cp>If an engineer ran a command that took down production, the useful question is why a single command could do that without a confirmation, a dry run, a limited blast radius or a review. Fix the system and the next person is protected, whoever they are.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Accountability remains. It shows up as owned, tracked action items, not as individual blame.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structure-of-a-postmortem\">Structure of a postmortem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Section\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Content\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Summary\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Two or three sentences: what broke, for how long, who was affected\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Impact\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Users, requests, revenue, SLO budget consumed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Timeline\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Timestamped events: detection, escalations, actions, recovery\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Root causes and contributing factors\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The chain of conditions that allowed the incident\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>What went well / badly / where we got lucky\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Honest reflection, including luck\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Action items\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Concrete, owned, dated changes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Lessons learned\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What others can learn\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Write it within a few days while memories are fresh. For SEV1 and SEV2 incidents, hold a short review meeting, and publish the document widely so other teams learn from it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"finding-causes-without-stopping-early\">Finding causes without stopping early\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a &quot;five whys&quot; style chain, but treat it as a way to explore branches, not to find one &quot;root cause&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Why did checkout fail?            The inventory service returned errors.\nWhy did it return errors?         Its database connection pool was exhausted.\nWhy was the pool exhausted?       A new query held connections for 6 seconds.\nWhy did the query take 6 seconds? A missing index on a new column.\nWhy did it reach production?      Load tests used a small dataset; no query-plan check in CI.\nWhy was there no alert earlier?   No saturation alert on connection pools.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Why did checkout fail?            The inventory service returned errors.\nWhy did it return errors?         Its database connection pool was exhausted.\nWhy was the pool exhausted?       A new query held connections for 6 seconds.\nWhy did the query take 6 seconds? A missing index on a new column.\nWhy did it reach production?      Load tests used a small dataset; no query-plan check in CI.\nWhy was there no alert earlier?   No saturation alert on connection pools.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each answer reveals a gap you can close: a missing index, a test that did not reflect production data, and missing monitoring. Stopping at &quot;a developer forgot an index&quot; would have fixed nothing systemic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"action-items-that-get-done\">Action items that get done\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Weak: &quot;Improve monitoring.&quot; Strong:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Owner\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Due\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add an alert for connection pool saturation above 80% for 5 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Detect\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Dana\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2026-11-07\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add a CI step that fails on queries with full table scans in test\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Prevent\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Arun\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2026-11-21\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Document the rollback steps in the runbook and rehearse once\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mitigate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mei\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2026-11-14\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prefer actions that prevent, detect or reduce impact over &quot;be more careful&quot;, which is not a control. Track them in the same system as your regular work, and review completion. A postmortem whose actions are never done is theatre.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"toil\">Toil\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Toil\u003c/strong> is work that is manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical (reactive), without lasting value, and that grows as the service grows. Restarting a stuck job by hand every week, manually creating accounts, copying certificates: all toil.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Not toil: engineering projects, design, and the overhead of meetings and planning. Toil is specifically the repetitive operational work that a machine could do.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reducing toil\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Automate the task\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A script or controller replaces the manual procedure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Remove the cause\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fix the bug that makes the job stick, rather than restarting it\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Self-service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Give developers a tool to do the task without a ticket\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Stop doing it\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Some tasks provide no value\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A common guideline is to keep toil \u003cstrong>below about half\u003c/strong> of an operations engineer&#39;s time, and to put the rest into engineering that reduces future toil. Measure it: ask the team to tag time spent, and rank sources of toil by hours per month. Automate the biggest first, and where automation costs more than the toil will ever, do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Blameless postmortems and toil fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blameless does not mean\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Structure of a postmortem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Finding causes without stopping\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Action items that get done\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Toil\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Blameless does not mean to Structure of a postmortem; 2, Structure of a postmortem to Finding causes without stopping; 3, Finding causes without stopping to Action items that get done; 4, Action items that get done to Toil.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Blameless does not mean \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Complex systems fail because of many contributing factors, not a single careless person.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Naming a person as the cause.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Structure of a postmortem \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write it within a few days while memories are fresh.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stopping at one &quot;root cause&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Finding causes without stopping \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use a &quot;five whys&quot; style chain, but treat it as a way to explore branches, not to find one &quot;root cause&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Finding causes without stopping early Use a &quot;five whys&quot; style chain, but treat it as a way to explore branches, not to find one &quot;root...\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Action items that get done \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Weak: &quot;Improve monitoring.&quot; Strong.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague actions with no owner or date.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Toil \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Toil is work that is manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical (reactive), without lasting value, and that grows as the service grows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Letting toil crowd out all engineering time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-same-outage-three-times\">Incident: the same outage three times\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An expired TLS certificate takes down the customer portal. It is the third certificate expiry in 18 months. The previous two postmortems each recorded the action &quot;be more careful with renewals&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The action items never changed the system, so the system produced the same result. Review the earlier documents and look for what a real fix would have been:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect\u003c/strong>: alert when any certificate expires in under 21 days, from an external check of the live endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prevent\u003c/strong>: automate issuance and renewal so no human is in the loop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Reduce impact\u003c/strong>: inventory every certificate and its owner\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo | openssl s_client -connect portal.example.com:443 -servername portal.example.com 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> | openssl s_client -connect portal.example.com:443 -servername portal.example.com 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notAfter=Nov 11 09:00:00 2026 GMT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A scheduled check like this one, run against every public hostname, gives early warning. Replace the vague action with the three concrete ones, give them owners and dates, and add the certificate renewal to the toil list with a target of zero manual steps. The fourth occurrence never happens.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Naming a person as the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping at one &quot;root cause&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vague actions with no owner or date\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing the postmortem and never reading it again\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting toil crowd out all engineering time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"blameless-does-not-mean-accountability-free","text":"Blameless does not mean accountability-free"},{"id":"structure-of-a-postmortem","text":"Structure of a postmortem"},{"id":"finding-causes-without-stopping-early","text":"Finding causes without stopping early"},{"id":"action-items-that-get-done","text":"Action items that get done"},{"id":"toil","text":"Toil"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-same-outage-three-times","text":"Incident: the same outage three times"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"SRE book: eliminating toil","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/"},{"t":"PagerDuty incident response documentation","u":"https://response.pagerduty.com/"}],"text":"An incident that teaches nothing will repeat. The postmortem turns a bad night into lasting improvement, but only if people feel safe describing what actually happened. And between incidents, the daily drudgery called toil quietly consumes the time you would need to prevent the next one. What you will learn What blameless means and why it matters The structure of a useful postmortem Writing action items that get done Defining and reducing toil Blameless does not mean accountability-free Complex systems fail because of many contributing factors, not a single careless person. If people are punished for mistakes, they hide them, and the organization loses the knowledge it needs. Blameless means we ask \"how did our systems, processes and tooling make this mistake easy and its impact large?\" rather than \"who did it?\" Why it matters If an engineer ran a command that took down production, the useful question is why a single command could do that without a confirmation, a dry run, a limited blast radius or a review. Fix the system and the next person is protected, whoever they are. Accountability remains. It shows up as owned, tracked action items, not as individual blame. Structure of a postmortem Section Content Summary Two or three sentences: what broke, for how long, who was affected Impact Users, requests, revenue, SLO budget consumed Timeline Timestamped events: detection, escalations, actions, recovery Root causes and contributing factors The chain of conditions that allowed the incident What went well / badly / where we got lucky Honest reflection, including luck Action items Concrete, owned, dated changes Lessons learned What others can learn Write it within a few days while memories are fresh. For SEV1 and SEV2 incidents, hold a short review meeting, and publish the document widely so other teams learn from it. Finding causes without stopping early Use a \"five whys\" style chain, but treat it as a way to explore branches, not to find one \"root cause\". Why did checkout fail? The inventory service returned errors. Why did it return errors? Its database connection pool was exhausted. Why was the pool exhausted? A new query held connections for 6 seconds. Why did the query take 6 seconds? A missing index on a new column. Why did it reach production? Load tests used a small dataset; no query-plan check in CI. Why was there no alert earlier? No saturation alert on connection pools. Each answer reveals a gap you can close: a missing index, a test that did not reflect production data, and missing monitoring. Stopping at \"a developer forgot an index\" would have fixed nothing systemic. Action items that get done Weak: \"Improve monitoring.\" Strong: Action Type Owner Due Add an alert for connection pool saturation above 80% for 5 minutes Detect Dana 2026-11-07 Add a CI step that fails on queries with full table scans in test Prevent Arun 2026-11-21 Document the rollback steps in the runbook and rehearse once Mitigate Mei 2026-11-14 Prefer actions that prevent, detect or reduce impact over \"be more careful\", which is not a control. Track them in the same system as your regular work, and review completion. A postmortem whose actions are never done is theatre. Toil Toil is work that is manual, repetitive, automatable, tactical (reactive), without lasting value, and that grows as the service grows. Restarting a stuck job by hand every week, manually creating accounts, copying certificates: all toil. Not toil: engineering projects, design, and the overhead of meetings and planning. Toil is specifically the repetitive operational work that a machine could do. Reducing toil Example Automate the task A script or controller replaces the manual procedure Remove the cause Fix the bug that makes the job stick, rather than restarting it Self-service Give developers a tool to do the task without a ticket Stop doing it Some tasks provide no value A common guideline is to keep toil below about half of an operations engineer&#39;s time, and to put the re"},"sre/reliability-patterns":{"html":"\u003cp>Large systems are always partly broken. A disk fails somewhere, a dependency slows down, a deploy goes wrong. Reliable systems are not those where nothing fails, but those designed so that failures stay small. A handful of patterns do most of the work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Timeouts, retries and the retry storm\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Circuit breakers and bulkheads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load shedding and graceful degradation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity planning and testing failure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts\">Timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every call over a network needs a \u003cstrong>timeout\u003c/strong>. Without one, a slow dependency holds your threads or connections indefinitely, and your service becomes slow, then dead, because of someone else&#39;s problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set timeouts shorter than the caller&#39;s own deadline. If the user&#39;s request has 2 seconds, do not make a 10-second call inside it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Propagate a \u003cstrong>deadline\u003c/strong> down the call chain, so downstream work is abandoned when the caller has already given up.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retries-with-care\">Retries, with care\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retrying helps with brief, transient failures. Done badly, it multiplies load on a struggling service and turns a hiccup into an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rules for safe retries:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Rule\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Reason\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Retry only \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong> operations (or use idempotency keys)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A retried payment must not charge twice\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Use \u003cstrong>exponential backoff\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Waiting 1s, 2s, 4s gives the system room to recover\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Add \u003cstrong>jitter\u003c/strong> (randomness)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Prevents all clients retrying at the same instant\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cap attempts (two or three) and total time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Avoids runaway work\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Use a \u003cstrong>retry budget\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Allow retries as at most about 10% of traffic, so they cannot swamp the service\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import random, time\n\ndef call_with_retries(fn, attempts=3, base=0.2, cap=2.0):\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except TransientError:\n            if attempt == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            sleep = min(cap, base * (2 ** attempt))\n            time.sleep(random.uniform(0, sleep))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> random, time\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">call_with_retries\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">fn, attempts=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, base=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.2\u003c/span>, cap=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2.0\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> attempt \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">range\u003c/span>(attempts):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">try\u003c/span>:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> fn()\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">except\u003c/span> TransientError:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> attempt == attempts - \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>:\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span>\n            sleep = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">min\u003c/span>(cap, base * (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> ** attempt))\n            time.sleep(random.uniform(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, sleep))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>If every layer of a five-layer system retries three times, one failing call at the bottom becomes 3 to the power 5, or 243 calls. Retry at one layer, usually the one closest to the user, and not at every layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"circuit-breakers\">Circuit breakers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>circuit breaker\u003c/strong> watches calls to a dependency. When failures pass a threshold, it \u003cstrong>opens\u003c/strong> and fails fast instead of calling, giving the dependency time to recover and saving your resources. After a pause it lets a few trial requests through (\u003cstrong>half-open\u003c/strong>), and closes again if they succeed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"closed (normal) --failures exceed threshold--&gt; open (fail fast)\nopen --after cool-down--&gt; half-open (trial requests)\nhalf-open --success--&gt; closed      half-open --failure--&gt; open\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">closed (normal) --failures exceed threshold--&gt; open (fail fast)\nopen --after cool-down--&gt; half-open (trial requests)\nhalf-open --success--&gt; closed      half-open --failure--&gt; open\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pair a breaker with a \u003cstrong>fallback\u003c/strong>: a cached value, a default, or a reduced feature.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bulkheads-and-graceful-degradation\">Bulkheads and graceful degradation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>bulkhead\u003c/strong> isolates resources, as a ship&#39;s compartments keep one leak from sinking it. Give each dependency its own connection pool or thread pool, so a slow recommendations service cannot use all your capacity and starve checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Graceful degradation\u003c/strong> means a failure in a non-critical feature should not break the critical path. If recommendations fail, show the product without them. Decide in advance which features are essential and which are optional.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"load-shedding-and-limits\">Load shedding and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When demand exceeds capacity, doing nothing makes every request slow and then fail. It is better to \u003cstrong>shed load\u003c/strong>: reject some requests quickly (HTTP 429 or 503 with a \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> header) so the rest succeed. Prioritize: keep checkout, shed bulk exports. Add \u003cstrong>rate limits\u003c/strong> per client so one noisy caller cannot harm others, and \u003cstrong>queues with bounded length\u003c/strong> so overload becomes visible rather than hidden latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-and-testing-failure\">Capacity and testing failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Know your limits with \u003cstrong>load tests\u003c/strong> that match production traffic shapes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep headroom: plan for peak plus the loss of one zone or instance group.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watch saturation trends, and add capacity before the limit, not at it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Practise failure.\u003c/strong> Game days and chaos experiments (kill an instance, add latency to a dependency, fail over a database) reveal what breaks while you are watching and ready, not at 3 a.m.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Start small: pick a hypothesis (&quot;if the cache is unavailable, checkout still works, only slower&quot;), limit the blast radius, run it in a quiet period, and stop immediately if customer impact appears.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Reliability patterns fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Timeouts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retries, with care\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Circuit breakers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bulkheads and graceful degradation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load shedding and limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity and testing failure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Timeouts to Retries, with care; 2, Retries, with care to Circuit breakers; 3, Circuit breakers to Bulkheads and graceful degradation; 4, Bulkheads and graceful degradation to Load shedding and limits; 5, Load shedding and limits to Capacity and testing failure.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Timeouts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every call over a network needs a timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retries at every layer, without backoff or jitter.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Retries, with care \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retrying helps with brief, transient failures.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying non-idempotent operations.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Circuit breakers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A circuit breaker watches calls to a dependency.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeouts on outbound calls.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Bulkheads and graceful degradation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A bulkhead isolates resources, as a ship's compartments keep one leak from sinking it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One shared pool for all dependencies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Load shedding and limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When demand exceeds capacity, doing nothing makes every request slow and then fail.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Load shedding and limits When demand exceeds capacity, doing nothing makes every request slow and then fail.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Capacity and testing failure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Know your limits with load tests that match production traffic shapes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never testing failure on purpose.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-retry-storm-after-a-short-blip\">Incident: a retry storm after a short blip\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The inventory database has a 20-second hiccup. Inventory recovers, but the system stays down for 40 minutes with the API at 100% CPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pod -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pod -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                   CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-a   1990m        480Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Charting the request rate that reached inventory shows what happened:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sum(rate(http_requests_total{service=&quot;inventory&quot;}[1m]))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;inventory&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>m]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rate was about 900 per second before the blip and 11,000 per second during it. During the blip, every client retried immediately and without limit. Inventory&#39;s request rate rose twelvefold, so when the database came back it was instantly overwhelmed again, and again. The retries \u003cem>were\u003c/em> the outage. Break the loop first: shed load at the edge or temporarily reduce traffic so inventory can recover (scaling the API further would only add more retrying clients). Then fix the design: exponential backoff with jitter, a cap of two attempts, a retry budget, a circuit breaker on the inventory client, and a fallback that serves cached stock levels. Rehearse the scenario in a game day by injecting latency into inventory and confirming the system stays stable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No timeouts on outbound calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries at every layer, without backoff or jitter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying non-idempotent operations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One shared pool for all dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing failure on purpose\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"timeouts","text":"Timeouts"},{"id":"retries-with-care","text":"Retries, with care"},{"id":"circuit-breakers","text":"Circuit breakers"},{"id":"bulkheads-and-graceful-degradation","text":"Bulkheads and graceful degradation"},{"id":"load-shedding-and-limits","text":"Load shedding and limits"},{"id":"capacity-and-testing-failure","text":"Capacity and testing failure"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-retry-storm-after-a-short-blip","text":"Incident: a retry storm after a short blip"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: handling overload","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"},{"t":"SRE book: addressing cascading failures","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"},{"t":"AWS: disaster recovery of workloads","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws.html"}],"text":"Large systems are always partly broken. A disk fails somewhere, a dependency slows down, a deploy goes wrong. Reliable systems are not those where nothing fails, but those designed so that failures stay small. A handful of patterns do most of the work. What you will learn Timeouts, retries and the retry storm Circuit breakers and bulkheads Load shedding and graceful degradation Capacity planning and testing failure Timeouts Every call over a network needs a timeout . Without one, a slow dependency holds your threads or connections indefinitely, and your service becomes slow, then dead, because of someone else&#39;s problem. Set timeouts shorter than the caller&#39;s own deadline. If the user&#39;s request has 2 seconds, do not make a 10-second call inside it. Propagate a deadline down the call chain, so downstream work is abandoned when the caller has already given up. Retries, with care Retrying helps with brief, transient failures. Done badly, it multiplies load on a struggling service and turns a hiccup into an outage. Rules for safe retries: Rule Reason Retry only idempotent operations (or use idempotency keys) A retried payment must not charge twice Use exponential backoff Waiting 1s, 2s, 4s gives the system room to recover Add jitter (randomness) Prevents all clients retrying at the same instant Cap attempts (two or three) and total time Avoids runaway work Use a retry budget Allow retries as at most about 10% of traffic, so they cannot swamp the service import random, time def call_with_retries ( fn, attempts= 3 , base= 0.2 , cap= 2.0 ): for attempt in range (attempts): try : return fn() except TransientError: if attempt == attempts - 1 : raise sleep = min (cap, base * ( 2 ** attempt)) time.sleep(random.uniform( 0 , sleep)) Warning If every layer of a five-layer system retries three times, one failing call at the bottom becomes 3 to the power 5, or 243 calls. Retry at one layer, usually the one closest to the user, and not at every layer. Circuit breakers A circuit breaker watches calls to a dependency. When failures pass a threshold, it opens and fails fast instead of calling, giving the dependency time to recover and saving your resources. After a pause it lets a few trial requests through ( half-open ), and closes again if they succeed. closed (normal) --failures exceed threshold--> open (fail fast) open --after cool-down--> half-open (trial requests) half-open --success--> closed half-open --failure--> open Pair a breaker with a fallback : a cached value, a default, or a reduced feature. Bulkheads and graceful degradation A bulkhead isolates resources, as a ship&#39;s compartments keep one leak from sinking it. Give each dependency its own connection pool or thread pool, so a slow recommendations service cannot use all your capacity and starve checkout. Graceful degradation means a failure in a non-critical feature should not break the critical path. If recommendations fail, show the product without them. Decide in advance which features are essential and which are optional. Load shedding and limits When demand exceeds capacity, doing nothing makes every request slow and then fail. It is better to shed load : reject some requests quickly (HTTP 429 or 503 with a Retry-After header) so the rest succeed. Prioritize: keep checkout, shed bulk exports. Add rate limits per client so one noisy caller cannot harm others, and queues with bounded length so overload becomes visible rather than hidden latency. Capacity and testing failure Know your limits with load tests that match production traffic shapes. Keep headroom: plan for peak plus the loss of one zone or instance group. Watch saturation trends, and add capacity before the limit, not at it. Practise failure. Game days and chaos experiments (kill an instance, add latency to a dependency, fail over a database) reveal what breaks while you are watching and ready, not at 3 a.m. Start small: pick a hypothesis (\"if the cache is unavailable, checkout still works, only slower\""},"sre/on-call":{"html":"\u003cp>On-call is where reliability meets people. A rotation that wakes engineers every night burns them out, drives them to leave and ends up making the service less reliable. A good one pages rarely, gives the person on duty what they need, and treats every page as a defect to remove.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rotation design: size, handoff, escalation and compensation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What deserves a page and what does not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runbooks and the first fifteen minutes of a page\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring load and reducing it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"designing-the-rotation\">Designing the rotation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Size\u003c/strong>: with fewer than five or six people, shifts come around too often. Aim for one week on duty in six or more, with a secondary for backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Follow the sun\u003c/strong> where teams are spread across time zones, so nobody works nights routinely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Primary and secondary\u003c/strong>: if the primary does not acknowledge in five minutes, the page goes to the secondary, then to a manager\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Handoff\u003c/strong>: a short written summary at each change of shift, covering open incidents, flaky alerts and planned risky changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fairness\u003c/strong>: pay or time off for on-call work, and no heroics expected after a hard night\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Handoff, week of 2 June\nOpen: checkout latency ticket OPS-412, mitigated, fix due Wednesday\nNoisy: disk-usage alert on batch-3 fires nightly, ignore until OPS-418 lands\nRisky: database failover test scheduled Thursday 10:00 UTC\nRunbooks changed: payment-gateway-timeouts updated after last incident\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Handoff, week of 2 June\nOpen: checkout latency ticket OPS-412, mitigated, fix due Wednesday\nNoisy: disk-usage alert on batch-3 fires nightly, ignore until OPS-418 lands\nRisky: database failover test scheduled Thursday 10:00 UTC\nRunbooks changed: payment-gateway-timeouts updated after last incident\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-deserves-a-page\">What deserves a page\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A page interrupts a human, possibly at night. It should meet all of these tests:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Urgent\u003c/strong>: waiting until morning makes it worse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Actionable\u003c/strong>: a person can do something now\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Symptom-based\u003c/strong>: users are, or soon will be, affected\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Real\u003c/strong>: a false alarm rate low enough that people trust it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Everything else is a ticket or a dashboard. Page on symptoms such as error rate, latency and burn rate against your SLO, not on causes such as CPU at 85 percent. Alerts for causes belong in lower-severity channels, used to diagnose rather than wake anyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# page: the error budget is being consumed 14x too fast, over two windows\n(\n  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;shop-api&quot;,code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[5m]))\n  / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;shop-api&quot;}[5m]))\n) &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)\nand\n(\n  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;shop-api&quot;,code=~&quot;5..&quot;}[1h]))\n  / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;shop-api&quot;}[1h]))\n) &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># page: the error budget is being consumed 14x too fast, over two windows\u003c/span>\n(\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">code\u003c/span>=~\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;5..&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m]))\n  / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m]))\n) &gt; (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14.4\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.001\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">and\u003c/span>\n(\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">code\u003c/span>=~\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;5..&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>h]))\n  / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>h]))\n) &gt; (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14.4\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.001\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runbooks\">Runbooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each paging alert links to a runbook that works for a tired person who did not write the service. A good runbook has:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the alert means and what users experience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The first three things to check, as copy-paste commands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mitigations ranked by safety, such as rollback, failover and scale-up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Who to escalate to and when\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Links to dashboards and the previous incidents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api\nkubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop | tail -3\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop\ncurl -fsS https://status.example.com/api/v2/status.json | jq -r '.status.description'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS https://status.example.com/api/v2/status.json | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.status.description&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Treat runbooks as code: keep them in version control, review them, and update them in the same change as the system they describe. Even better, automate the safe steps so the runbook says &quot;run this command&quot; or the system does it unattended.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-first-fifteen-minutes\">The first fifteen minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Acknowledge\u003c/strong> so others know someone owns it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Assess\u003c/strong>: how bad, who is affected, getting worse?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Escalate early\u003c/strong> if you are unsure: declare an incident and call the secondary. Asking for help in the first five minutes is not weakness\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Mitigate\u003c/strong> before diagnosing, using the runbook\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Communicate\u003c/strong> on a fixed rhythm\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-and-reducing-load\">Measuring and reducing load\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track per rotation: pages per shift, pages outside working hours, time to acknowledge, and the share of pages that needed no action. A common guideline from SRE practice is no more than two incidents per shift, since each needs time to handle and to follow up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -H &quot;Authorization: Token token=$PD_TOKEN&quot; &quot;https://api.pagerduty.com/analytics/raw/incidents&quot; | jq '.data | length'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Token token=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PD_TOKEN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.pagerduty.com/analytics/raw/incidents&quot;\u003c/span> | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.data | length&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Hold a weekly review of every page: Was it actionable? Could automation have fixed it? Should the alert change? Delete or downgrade alerts that nobody acted on. Pages that repeat belong in the toil backlog with an owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Running a healthy on-call rotation fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Designing the rotation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What deserves a page\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runbooks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The first fifteen minutes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measuring and reducing load\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Designing the rotation to What deserves a page; 2, What deserves a page to Runbooks; 3, Runbooks to The first fifteen minutes; 4, The first fifteen minutes to Measuring and reducing load.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Designing the rotation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Size: with fewer than five or six people, shifts come around too often.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerts with no runbook or no clear owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>What deserves a page \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A page interrupts a human, possibly at night.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Paging on causes such as CPU and memory instead of symptoms.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Runbooks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each paging alert links to a runbook that works for a tired person who did not write the service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blaming the individual for missing a page in a noisy system.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>The first fifteen minutes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Acknowledge so others know someone owns it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Measuring and reducing load \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Track per rotation: pages per shift, pages outside working hours, time to acknowledge, and the share of pages that needed no action.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Nobody reviewing pages, so noise only grows.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-alert-that-cried-wolf\">Incident: the alert that cried wolf\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Over three months the team&#39;s pager has grown to 40 pages a week, mostly disk alerts at 80 percent that clear themselves. A real database failover alert fires at 02:10 and the on-call engineer, having been woken four times already that week by false alarms, silences it and goes back to sleep. Orders fail until 06:00.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Alert fatigue made the real signal look like the usual noise. Fix the system rather than the person:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get prometheusrules -A -o json | jq -r '.items[].spec.groups[].rules[] | select(.alert) | .alert' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get prometheusrules -A -o json | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[].spec.groups[].rules[] | select(.alert) | .alert&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -rn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>List every alert, review which ones led to action, and delete or downgrade the rest. Replace cause-based alerts with SLO burn-rate alerts. Add automatic disk clean-up for the repeating one, set quiet hours only for non-urgent notifications, and make a rule that any alert ignored three times must be fixed or removed that week.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Paging on causes such as CPU and memory instead of symptoms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts with no runbook or no clear owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A rotation too small, so people are always on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Nobody reviewing pages, so noise only grows\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blaming the individual for missing a page in a noisy system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"designing-the-rotation","text":"Designing the rotation"},{"id":"what-deserves-a-page","text":"What deserves a page"},{"id":"runbooks","text":"Runbooks"},{"id":"the-first-fifteen-minutes","text":"The first fifteen minutes"},{"id":"measuring-and-reducing-load","text":"Measuring and reducing load"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-alert-that-cried-wolf","text":"Incident: the alert that cried wolf"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: being on-call","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/being-on-call/"},{"t":"PagerDuty incident response documentation","u":"https://response.pagerduty.com/"},{"t":"SRE book: practical alerting","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/practical-alerting/"}],"text":"On-call is where reliability meets people. A rotation that wakes engineers every night burns them out, drives them to leave and ends up making the service less reliable. A good one pages rarely, gives the person on duty what they need, and treats every page as a defect to remove. What you will learn Rotation design: size, handoff, escalation and compensation What deserves a page and what does not Runbooks and the first fifteen minutes of a page Measuring load and reducing it Designing the rotation Size : with fewer than five or six people, shifts come around too often. Aim for one week on duty in six or more, with a secondary for backup Follow the sun where teams are spread across time zones, so nobody works nights routinely Primary and secondary : if the primary does not acknowledge in five minutes, the page goes to the secondary, then to a manager Handoff : a short written summary at each change of shift, covering open incidents, flaky alerts and planned risky changes Fairness : pay or time off for on-call work, and no heroics expected after a hard night Handoff, week of 2 June Open: checkout latency ticket OPS-412, mitigated, fix due Wednesday Noisy: disk-usage alert on batch-3 fires nightly, ignore until OPS-418 lands Risky: database failover test scheduled Thursday 10:00 UTC Runbooks changed: payment-gateway-timeouts updated after last incident What deserves a page A page interrupts a human, possibly at night. It should meet all of these tests: Urgent : waiting until morning makes it worse Actionable : a person can do something now Symptom-based : users are, or soon will be, affected Real : a false alarm rate low enough that people trust it Everything else is a ticket or a dashboard. Page on symptoms such as error rate, latency and burn rate against your SLO, not on causes such as CPU at 85 percent. Alerts for causes belong in lower-severity channels, used to diagnose rather than wake anyone. # page: the error budget is being consumed 14x too fast, over two windows ( sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"shop-api\" , code =~ \"5..\" }[ 5 m])) / sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"shop-api\" }[ 5 m])) ) > ( 14.4 * 0.001 ) and ( sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"shop-api\" , code =~ \"5..\" }[ 1 h])) / sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"shop-api\" }[ 1 h])) ) > ( 14.4 * 0.001 ) Runbooks Each paging alert links to a runbook that works for a tired person who did not write the service. A good runbook has: What the alert means and what users experience The first three things to check, as copy-paste commands Mitigations ranked by safety, such as rollback, failover and scale-up Who to escalate to and when Links to dashboards and the previous incidents $ kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api $ kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop | tail -3 $ kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop $ curl -fsS https://status.example.com/api/v2/status.json | jq -r &#x27;.status.description&#x27; Treat runbooks as code: keep them in version control, review them, and update them in the same change as the system they describe. Even better, automate the safe steps so the runbook says \"run this command\" or the system does it unattended. The first fifteen minutes Acknowledge so others know someone owns it Assess : how bad, who is affected, getting worse? Escalate early if you are unsure: declare an incident and call the secondary. Asking for help in the first five minutes is not weakness Mitigate before diagnosing, using the runbook Communicate on a fixed rhythm Measuring and reducing load Track per rotation: pages per shift, pages outside working hours, time to acknowledge, and the share of pages that needed no action. A common guideline from SRE practice is no more than two incidents per shift, since each needs time to handle and to follow up. $ curl -s -H \"Authorization: Token token= $PD_TOKEN \" \"https://api.pagerduty.com/analytics/raw/incidents\" | jq &#x27;.data | length&#x27; Hold a weekly review of every page: Was"},"sre/capacity-planning":{"html":"\u003cp>Running out of capacity is an avoidable outage. Having too much wastes money. Capacity planning is the discipline of knowing how much load your system can carry, how fast demand is growing and how long it takes to add more.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Demand forecasting from real traffic data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load, stress and soak testing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Headroom, N+1 and failure-domain planning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lead times, autoscaling and cost\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-your-demand\">Know your demand\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with measurements: requests per second (peak, not average), growth over months, and seasonal events such as sales or month-end processing. Use Prometheus to see the trend:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"max_over_time(sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=&quot;shop-api&quot;}[5m]))[7d:5m])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">max_over_time\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rate\u003c/span>(http_requests_total{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m]))[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>d:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>m])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"promql\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=&quot;/data&quot;}[7d], 14 * 24 * 3600) &lt; 0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">predict_linear\u003c/span>(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountpoint\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/data&quot;\u003c/span>}[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>d], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">24\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3600\u003c/span>) &lt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The second query forecasts when a disk will fill, based on the last week of growth. Combine technical trends with business input: product launches, marketing campaigns and customer onboarding all change demand in ways history cannot show.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-limits\">Find the limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You cannot plan capacity without knowing what one unit can handle. Test it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Test\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question it answers\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Load\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Does the system meet its SLO at the expected peak?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Stress\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>At what load does it break, and how does it fail?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Soak\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Does it stay healthy for hours? (finds leaks)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Spike\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can it handle a sudden jump?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import http from 'k6/http';\nimport { check } from 'k6';\n\nexport const options = {\n  stages: [\n    { duration: '2m', target: 100 },\n    { duration: '5m', target: 100 },\n    { duration: '2m', target: 300 },\n    { duration: '5m', target: 300 },\n    { duration: '2m', target: 0 },\n  ],\n  thresholds: {\n    http_req_failed: ['rate&lt;0.01'],\n    http_req_duration: ['p(95)&lt;400'],\n  },\n};\n\nexport default function () {\n  const r = http.get('https://staging.example.com/api/products');\n  check(r, { ok: (res) =&gt; res.status === 200 });\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">import http from &#x27;k6/http&#x27;;\nimport { check } from &#x27;k6&#x27;;\n\nexport const options = {\n  stages: [\n    { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 100 },\n    { duration: &#x27;5m&#x27;, target: 100 },\n    { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 300 },\n    { duration: &#x27;5m&#x27;, target: 300 },\n    { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 0 },\n  ],\n  thresholds: {\n    http_req_failed: [&#x27;rate&lt;0.01&#x27;],\n    http_req_duration: [&#x27;p(95)&lt;400&#x27;],\n  },\n};\n\nexport default function () {\n  const r = http.get(&#x27;https://staging.example.com/api/products&#x27;);\n  check(r, { ok: (res) =&gt; res.status === 200 });\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run load tests against an environment that resembles production, never against production data without protection. Watch the saturation signals during the test: CPU, memory, connection pools, queue depth and database load. The first resource to saturate is the bottleneck, and the number to remember is the throughput at which latency starts to exceed the SLO, not the point of total failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"k6 run loadtest.js\nkubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=cpu | head -5\nkubectl get hpa -n shop -w\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k6 run loadtest.js\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=cpu | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get hpa -n shop -w\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headroom-and-redundancy\">Headroom and redundancy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If one instance handles 200 requests per second at the SLO, and peak demand is 900, you need five instances for the load. Add:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Headroom\u003c/strong> for growth and bursts, commonly 20 to 30 percent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>N+1 or N+2\u003c/strong> so that losing one node or one zone does not take you over capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Failure-domain thinking\u003c/strong>: with three zones, losing one leaves 67 percent of capacity, so each zone must be sized to carry half the peak alone\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"peak load            900 rps\nper-instance limit   200 rps at SLO\nneeded for load      5 instances\nwith 25% headroom    6 instances\nsurvive loss of 1 of 3 zones (2 zones carry all): 3 per zone = 9 instances\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">peak load            900 rps\nper-instance limit   200 rps at SLO\nneeded for load      5 instances\nwith 25% headroom    6 instances\nsurvive loss of 1 of 3 zones (2 zones carry all): 3 per zone = 9 instances\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last line shows why &quot;survive a zone failure&quot; is the most expensive requirement and deserves an explicit decision rather than an accident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lead-time-and-autoscaling\">Lead time and autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Capacity must arrive before demand. Ask how long it takes to add some: autoscaling takes minutes, ordering hardware or a large cloud commitment takes weeks, and database sharding takes months. Plan based on the \u003cstrong>longest lead time\u003c/strong> among the resources a service depends on.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Autoscaling covers short-term variation, but it does not remove the need for planning: quotas, subnet addresses, database connections and downstream services all have limits that autoscaling will hit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws service-quotas get-service-quota --service-code ec2 --quota-code L-1216C47A --query 'Quota.Value'\nkubectl describe quota -n shop\naws ec2 describe-subnets --subnet-ids subnet-0abc --query 'Subnets[0].AvailableIpAddressCount'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws service-quotas get-service-quota --service-code ec2 --quota-code L-1216C47A --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Quota.Value&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe quota -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-subnets --subnet-ids subnet-0abc --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Subnets[0].AvailableIpAddressCount&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-and-cost\">Capacity and cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review utilisation regularly. Persistently low usage means over-provisioning, and persistently high usage means risk. Use commitments such as reserved capacity or savings plans for the steady base, on-demand or spot for the variable part, and tag resources so cost is attributed to the teams who control it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Capacity planning and performance testing fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Know your demand\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Find the limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Headroom and redundancy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lead time and autoscaling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity and cost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Know your demand to Find the limits; 2, Find the limits to Headroom and redundancy; 3, Headroom and redundancy to Lead time and autoscaling; 4, Lead time and autoscaling to Capacity and cost.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Know your demand \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with measurements: requests per second (peak, not average), growth over months, and seasonal events such as sales or month-end processing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forecasting only from history, with no input from the business.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Find the limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> You cannot plan capacity without knowing what one unit can handle.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Planning for average load instead of peak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Headroom and redundancy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If one instance handles 200 requests per second at the SLO, and peak demand is 900, you need five instances for the load.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No headroom or N+1, so one failure causes an overload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Lead time and autoscaling \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Capacity must arrive before demand. Ask how long it takes to add some: autoscaling takes minutes, ordering hardware or a large cloud commitment takes weeks, and database...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring quotas and address space limits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Capacity and cost \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Review utilisation regularly. Persistently low usage means over-provisioning, and persistently high usage means risk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-launch-day-that-overwhelmed-the-database\">Incident: the launch day that overwhelmed the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Marketing launches a campaign without telling engineering. Web servers autoscale from 10 to 60 instances within minutes, but the database hits its connection limit of 500, queries queue up, and every instance starts failing health checks. The site is down for the launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Autoscaling the stateless tier moved the bottleneck to the shared database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -h db.internal -c &quot;select count(*) from pg_stat_activity;&quot;\npsql -h db.internal -c &quot;show max_connections;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h db.internal -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from pg_stat_activity;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   498\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h db.internal -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;show max_connections;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Immediate mitigation: cap the Deployment&#39;s maximum replicas, shed load with a queue or cached pages, and scale the database or add a connection pooler such as PgBouncer. Long term: load-test the whole path including dependencies, set autoscaling maximums from the weakest dependency, and add a launch checklist that gives engineering notice of expected traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Planning for average load instead of peak\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing only the stateless tier\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No headroom or N+1, so one failure causes an overload\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring quotas and address space limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasting only from history, with no input from the business\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"know-your-demand","text":"Know your demand"},{"id":"find-the-limits","text":"Find the limits"},{"id":"headroom-and-redundancy","text":"Headroom and redundancy"},{"id":"lead-time-and-autoscaling","text":"Lead time and autoscaling"},{"id":"capacity-and-cost","text":"Capacity and cost"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-launch-day-that-overwhelmed-the-database","text":"Incident: the launch day that overwhelmed the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: reliable product launches","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/reliable-product-launches/"},{"t":"Grafana k6 documentation","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Horizontal Pod Autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"}],"text":"Running out of capacity is an avoidable outage. Having too much wastes money. Capacity planning is the discipline of knowing how much load your system can carry, how fast demand is growing and how long it takes to add more. What you will learn Demand forecasting from real traffic data Load, stress and soak testing Headroom, N+1 and failure-domain planning Lead times, autoscaling and cost Know your demand Start with measurements: requests per second (peak, not average), growth over months, and seasonal events such as sales or month-end processing. Use Prometheus to see the trend: max_over_time ( sum ( rate (http_requests_total{ job = \"shop-api\" }[ 5 m]))[ 7 d: 5 m]) predict_linear (node_filesystem_avail_bytes{ mountpoint = \"/data\" }[ 7 d], 14 * 24 * 3600 ) \u003c 0 The second query forecasts when a disk will fill, based on the last week of growth. Combine technical trends with business input: product launches, marketing campaigns and customer onboarding all change demand in ways history cannot show. Find the limits You cannot plan capacity without knowing what one unit can handle. Test it. Test Question it answers Load Does the system meet its SLO at the expected peak? Stress At what load does it break, and how does it fail? Soak Does it stay healthy for hours? (finds leaks) Spike Can it handle a sudden jump? import http from &#x27;k6/http&#x27;; import { check } from &#x27;k6&#x27;; export const options = { stages: [ { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 100 }, { duration: &#x27;5m&#x27;, target: 100 }, { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 300 }, { duration: &#x27;5m&#x27;, target: 300 }, { duration: &#x27;2m&#x27;, target: 0 }, ], thresholds: { http_req_failed: [&#x27;rate\u003c0.01&#x27;], http_req_duration: [&#x27;p(95)\u003c400&#x27;], }, }; export default function () { const r = http.get(&#x27;https://staging.example.com/api/products&#x27;); check(r, { ok: (res) => res.status === 200 }); } Run load tests against an environment that resembles production, never against production data without protection. Watch the saturation signals during the test: CPU, memory, connection pools, queue depth and database load. The first resource to saturate is the bottleneck, and the number to remember is the throughput at which latency starts to exceed the SLO, not the point of total failure. $ k6 run loadtest.js $ kubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=cpu | head -5 $ kubectl get hpa -n shop -w Headroom and redundancy If one instance handles 200 requests per second at the SLO, and peak demand is 900, you need five instances for the load. Add: Headroom for growth and bursts, commonly 20 to 30 percent N+1 or N+2 so that losing one node or one zone does not take you over capacity Failure-domain thinking : with three zones, losing one leaves 67 percent of capacity, so each zone must be sized to carry half the peak alone peak load 900 rps per-instance limit 200 rps at SLO needed for load 5 instances with 25% headroom 6 instances survive loss of 1 of 3 zones (2 zones carry all): 3 per zone = 9 instances The last line shows why \"survive a zone failure\" is the most expensive requirement and deserves an explicit decision rather than an accident. Lead time and autoscaling Capacity must arrive before demand. Ask how long it takes to add some: autoscaling takes minutes, ordering hardware or a large cloud commitment takes weeks, and database sharding takes months. Plan based on the longest lead time among the resources a service depends on. Autoscaling covers short-term variation, but it does not remove the need for planning: quotas, subnet addresses, database connections and downstream services all have limits that autoscaling will hit. $ aws service-quotas get-service-quota --service-code ec2 --quota-code L-1216C47A --query &#x27;Quota.Value&#x27; $ kubectl describe quota -n shop $ aws ec2 describe-subnets --subnet-ids subnet-0abc --query &#x27;Subnets[0].AvailableIpAddressCount&#x27; Capacity and cost Review utilisation regularly. Persistently low usage means over-provi"},"sre/chaos-engineering-and-game-days":{"html":"\u003cp>Systems fail in ways nobody predicted, usually at the worst time. You can discover the weaknesses during a calm Tuesday afternoon with the whole team watching, or at 03:00 with customers watching. \u003cstrong>Chaos engineering\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>game days\u003c/strong> choose the first option.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The chaos experiment loop and how to keep it safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a game day\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools for injecting failure in Kubernetes and cloud\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disaster recovery: RTO, RPO and proving your backups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-experiment-loop\">The experiment loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Chaos engineering is not breaking things at random. It is the scientific method applied to production readiness.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Define steady state\u003c/strong>: measurable behaviour that means &quot;healthy&quot;, such as the checkout success rate and p95 latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Form a hypothesis\u003c/strong>: &quot;If one API Pod is killed, checkout success stays above 99.9 percent&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limit the blast radius\u003c/strong>: start small, in staging or on a slice of traffic, with a clear way to stop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Inject the failure\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Observe\u003c/strong> against the steady state, and compare with the hypothesis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Learn and fix\u003c/strong>, then repeat with a larger failure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>An experiment that confirms the hypothesis is a success: you have evidence of resilience. An experiment that fails is a bigger success, since you found the weakness cheaply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety-rules\">Safety rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Have a \u003cstrong>hypothesis, an owner and an abort condition\u003c/strong> before starting (for example &quot;stop if the error rate exceeds 2 percent&quot;)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Announce the experiment, and avoid running during incidents, releases or peak traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make sure monitoring and the stop button work first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Begin in non-production, then production with a small scope\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never test what you could not recover from\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"injecting-failure\">Injecting failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl delete pod -l app=shop-api -n shop --field-selector=status.phase=Running --wait=false | head -1\nkubectl get pods -n shop -w\nkubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nkubectl uncordon worker-3\nsudo tc qdisc add dev eth0 root netem delay 200ms loss 2%\nsudo tc qdisc del dev eth0 root\nstress-ng --cpu 4 --timeout 120s\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl delete pod -l app=shop-api -n shop --field-selector=status.phase=Running --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -w\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl uncordon worker-3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> tc qdisc add dev eth0 root netem delay 200ms loss 2%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> tc qdisc del dev eth0 root\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>stress-ng --cpu 4 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span> 120s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dedicated tools make experiments repeatable and safer. \u003cstrong>Chaos Mesh\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>LitmusChaos\u003c/strong> run experiments as Kubernetes resources, and cloud providers offer \u003cstrong>AWS Fault Injection Service\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Azure Chaos Studio\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1\nkind: PodChaos\nmetadata:\n  name: kill-one-api-pod\n  namespace: chaos-testing\nspec:\n  action: pod-kill\n  mode: one\n  selector:\n    namespaces: [&quot;shop&quot;]\n    labelSelectors:\n      app: shop-api\n  duration: &quot;60s&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PodChaos\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kill-one-api-pod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">chaos-testing\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">action:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pod-kill\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">one\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaces:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labelSelectors:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;60s&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Useful failures to rehearse: a Pod or node dying, a zone becoming unavailable, a dependency returning errors or becoming slow, DNS failing, a disk filling, a certificate expiring and an expired credential.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"game-days\">Game days\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>game day\u003c/strong> is a scheduled exercise where the team responds to a simulated failure, practising the human side: detection, communication, escalation and runbooks.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Pick a scenario drawn from past incidents or from a risk review, for example &quot;primary database fails over&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assign roles: a facilitator who injects events, the responders, an observer taking notes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run it: the facilitator reveals symptoms step by step, and responders use normal tools and runbooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debrief within a day: what worked, what was slow, what runbook was wrong?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Track fixes like any other work\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The result is rarely a technical finding alone. You will learn that the runbook refers to a dashboard that no longer exists, or that only one person knows the failover procedure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disaster-recovery\">Disaster recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two numbers define what you must achieve:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (recovery time objective): how long the service may be down\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (recovery point objective): how much data you may lose, measured in time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A backup strategy is only as good as its \u003cstrong>last successful restore test\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical RTO\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Backup and restore\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pilot light (core running, rest scaled down)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tens of minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Warm standby\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Higher\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active-active multi-region\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Near zero\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Highest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws rds describe-db-snapshots --db-instance-identifier shop-prod --query 'DBSnapshots[-1].[SnapshotCreateTime,Status]'\naws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot --db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test --db-snapshot-identifier shop-prod-2025-06-02\npsql -h shop-restore-test.abc.eu-west-1.rds.amazonaws.com -U app -c &quot;select count(*) from orders;&quot;\naws rds delete-db-instance --db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test --skip-final-snapshot\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds describe-db-snapshots --db-instance-identifier shop-prod --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;DBSnapshots[-1].[SnapshotCreateTime,Status]&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot --db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test --db-snapshot-identifier shop-prod-2025-06-02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h shop-restore-test.abc.eu-west-1.rds.amazonaws.com -U app -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from orders;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds delete-db-instance --db-instance-identifier shop-restore-test --skip-final-snapshot\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Automate this restore test on a schedule and alert if it fails. Measure the actual time and compare it with the RTO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Chaos engineering, game days and disaster recovery fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The experiment loop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Safety rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Injecting failure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Game days\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Disaster recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The experiment loop to Safety rules; 2, Safety rules to Injecting failure; 3, Injecting failure to Game days; 4, Game days to Disaster recovery.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The experiment loop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Chaos engineering is not breaking things at random.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Game days that nobody follows up with fixes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Safety rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Have a hypothesis, an owner and an abort condition before starting (for example &quot;stop if the error rate exceeds 2 percent&quot;).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Running experiments without a hypothesis or abort condition.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Injecting failure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dedicated tools make experiments repeatable and safer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Chaos testing before basic monitoring works.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Game days \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A game day is a scheduled exercise where the team responds to a simulated failure, practising the human side: detection, communication, escalation and runbooks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stating RTO and RPO that have never been measured.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Disaster recovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Two numbers define what you must achieve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating a successful backup job as proof of restorability.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-that-did-not-restore\">Incident: the backup that did not restore\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The primary database is corrupted by a faulty migration. The team starts the restore, and discovers the nightly backups have been empty files for five weeks because a credential expired and the job reported success anyway. The newest usable copy is from a month ago.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The problem is untested backups and a job that ignored its own errors. Recovery uses whatever remains:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 ls s3://acme-backups/shop/ --human-readable | tail -5\naws rds describe-db-snapshots --snapshot-type automated --query 'DBSnapshots[].[DBSnapshotIdentifier,SnapshotCreateTime]' --output text | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://acme-backups/shop/ --human-readable | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws rds describe-db-snapshots --snapshot-type automated --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;DBSnapshots[].[DBSnapshotIdentifier,SnapshotCreateTime]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Fall back to the cloud provider&#39;s automated snapshots and point-in-time recovery, accept the data loss that remains and tell stakeholders plainly. Then fix the process: backup jobs must fail loudly, a scheduled restore test must verify row counts, alerts must fire on backup size anomalies, and a quarterly game day must exercise the full restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running experiments without a hypothesis or abort condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chaos testing before basic monitoring works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a successful backup job as proof of restorability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Game days that nobody follows up with fixes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stating RTO and RPO that have never been measured\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-experiment-loop","text":"The experiment loop"},{"id":"safety-rules","text":"Safety rules"},{"id":"injecting-failure","text":"Injecting failure"},{"id":"game-days","text":"Game days"},{"id":"disaster-recovery","text":"Disaster recovery"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-that-did-not-restore","text":"Incident: the backup that did not restore"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Principles of Chaos Engineering","u":"https://principlesofchaos.org/"},{"t":"AWS Fault Injection Service","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/fis/latest/userguide/what-is.html"},{"t":"Azure Chaos Studio","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/chaos-studio/chaos-studio-overview"}],"text":"Systems fail in ways nobody predicted, usually at the worst time. You can discover the weaknesses during a calm Tuesday afternoon with the whole team watching, or at 03:00 with customers watching. Chaos engineering and game days choose the first option. What you will learn The chaos experiment loop and how to keep it safe Running a game day Tools for injecting failure in Kubernetes and cloud Disaster recovery: RTO, RPO and proving your backups The experiment loop Chaos engineering is not breaking things at random. It is the scientific method applied to production readiness. Define steady state : measurable behaviour that means \"healthy\", such as the checkout success rate and p95 latency Form a hypothesis : \"If one API Pod is killed, checkout success stays above 99.9 percent\" Limit the blast radius : start small, in staging or on a slice of traffic, with a clear way to stop Inject the failure Observe against the steady state, and compare with the hypothesis Learn and fix , then repeat with a larger failure An experiment that confirms the hypothesis is a success: you have evidence of resilience. An experiment that fails is a bigger success, since you found the weakness cheaply. Safety rules Have a hypothesis, an owner and an abort condition before starting (for example \"stop if the error rate exceeds 2 percent\") Announce the experiment, and avoid running during incidents, releases or peak traffic Make sure monitoring and the stop button work first Begin in non-production, then production with a small scope Never test what you could not recover from Injecting failure $ kubectl delete pod -l app=shop-api -n shop --field-selector=status.phase=Running -- wait = false | head -1 $ kubectl get pods -n shop -w $ kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data $ kubectl uncordon worker-3 $ sudo tc qdisc add dev eth0 root netem delay 200ms loss 2% $ sudo tc qdisc del dev eth0 root $ stress-ng --cpu 4 -- timeout 120s Dedicated tools make experiments repeatable and safer. Chaos Mesh and LitmusChaos run experiments as Kubernetes resources, and cloud providers offer AWS Fault Injection Service and Azure Chaos Studio . apiVersion: chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1 kind: PodChaos metadata: name: kill-one-api-pod namespace: chaos-testing spec: action: pod-kill mode: one selector: namespaces: [ \"shop\" ] labelSelectors: app: shop-api duration: \"60s\" Useful failures to rehearse: a Pod or node dying, a zone becoming unavailable, a dependency returning errors or becoming slow, DNS failing, a disk filling, a certificate expiring and an expired credential. Game days A game day is a scheduled exercise where the team responds to a simulated failure, practising the human side: detection, communication, escalation and runbooks. Pick a scenario drawn from past incidents or from a risk review, for example \"primary database fails over\" Assign roles: a facilitator who injects events, the responders, an observer taking notes Run it: the facilitator reveals symptoms step by step, and responders use normal tools and runbooks Debrief within a day: what worked, what was slow, what runbook was wrong? Track fixes like any other work The result is rarely a technical finding alone. You will learn that the runbook refers to a dashboard that no longer exists, or that only one person knows the failover procedure. Disaster recovery Two numbers define what you must achieve: RTO (recovery time objective): how long the service may be down RPO (recovery point objective): how much data you may lose, measured in time A backup strategy is only as good as its last successful restore test . Strategy Typical RTO Cost Backup and restore Hours Low Pilot light (core running, rest scaled down) Tens of minutes Medium Warm standby Minutes Higher Active-active multi-region Near zero Highest $ aws rds describe-db-snapshots --db-instance-identifier shop-prod --query &#x27;DBSnapshots[-1].[SnapshotCreateTime,Status]&#x27; $ aws rds restore-db-instance-from-db-snapshot --db-instance-id"},"sre/change-management-and-releases":{"html":"\u003cp>Most incidents begin with a change: a deployment, a configuration edit, a new dependency version, a migration. The goal of release engineering is not to stop change, because change is how a business improves, but to make each change small, observable and reversible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why small, frequent changes are safer than rare large ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Feature flags and dark launches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary releases with automated analysis and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Change windows, freezes and tying releases to the error budget\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"small-batches\">Small batches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A release of one change has one suspect when something breaks. A release of two hundred changes has two hundred. Frequent small releases make failures easy to locate and easy to revert. This is why teams with strong delivery practices both deploy more often \u003cstrong>and\u003c/strong> have lower failure rates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Track four delivery measures (the DORA metrics): \u003cstrong>deployment frequency\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>lead time for changes\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>change failure rate\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>time to restore service\u003c/strong>. Improving the first two should not worsen the last two, and if it does, the safety net needs work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"feature-flags\">Feature flags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>feature flag\u003c/strong> separates deploying code from releasing a feature. Code ships turned off, then is enabled gradually for a team, a percentage of users or a region, and can be switched off instantly without a deployment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def checkout(cart, user):\n    if flags.enabled(&quot;new-payment-flow&quot;, user=user.id, default=False):\n        return new_payment_flow(cart)\n    return legacy_payment_flow(cart)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">checkout\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">cart, user\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> flags.enabled(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-payment-flow&quot;\u003c/span>, user=user.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>, default=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> new_payment_flow(cart)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> legacy_payment_flow(cart)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"flags:\n  new-payment-flow:\n    enabled: true\n    rollout:\n      percentage: 5\n      allow: [&quot;internal-staff&quot;]\n    owner: payments-team\n    expires: 2025-09-30\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">flags:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">new-payment-flow:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rollout:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">percentage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allow:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;internal-staff&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">owner:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments-team\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expires:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2025-09-30\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Flags have costs: each one doubles the code paths to test. Give every flag an owner and an expiry date, and delete flags promptly after full rollout. Make sure the \u003cstrong>default\u003c/strong> is the safe behaviour when the flag service is unreachable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"canary-and-progressive-delivery\">Canary and progressive delivery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong> sends a small share of traffic to the new version and compares it with the stable version before continuing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Rollout\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  replicas: 10\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: shop-api\n  strategy:\n    canary:\n      steps:\n        - setWeight: 5\n        - pause: { duration: 5m }\n        - analysis:\n            templates:\n              - templateName: error-rate-and-latency\n        - setWeight: 25\n        - pause: { duration: 10m }\n        - setWeight: 50\n        - pause: { duration: 10m }\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: shop-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Rollout\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strategy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">canary:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pause:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5m\u003c/span> }\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">analysis:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">templates:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">templateName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">error-rate-and-latency\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">25\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pause:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10m\u003c/span> }\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">setWeight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pause:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">duration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10m\u003c/span> }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An analysis step queries your metrics, for example &quot;5xx rate of the canary is not more than 1.2 times the baseline and p95 latency is under 400 ms&quot;. If the check fails, the rollout \u003cstrong>aborts and reverts automatically\u003c/strong>. This depends entirely on trustworthy metrics, so build the SLO dashboards first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl argo rollouts get rollout shop-api -n shop --watch\nkubectl argo rollouts abort shop-api -n shop\nkubectl argo rollouts undo shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl argo rollouts get rollout shop-api -n shop --watch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl argo rollouts abort shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl argo rollouts undo shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"database-and-configuration-changes\">Database and configuration changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Schema changes are the hardest to roll back. Use \u003cstrong>expand and contract\u003c/strong>: add new columns or tables first (compatible with old code), deploy the code that uses them, migrate the data, and only later remove the old structure. Never ship a migration and the code requiring it as one irreversible step. Treat configuration as code: reviewed, versioned, validated, and rolled out through the same pipeline and canary process.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"change-policy-and-the-error-budget\">Change policy and the error budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tie release behaviour to your reliability data:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>While the SLO is met, ship freely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When the error budget is nearly spent, slow down, reduce risk and prioritise reliability work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When it is exhausted, freeze non-essential changes until it recovers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A freeze at peak business times (a sales event, month-end) is reasonable, but a freeze is a blunt tool. Prefer to make releases safer over making them rarer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"peer-review-and-risk\">Peer review and risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review proportionate to risk. A copy change needs less ceremony than a database migration. Ask of every risky change: What is the blast radius? How will we know it is wrong? How do we undo it, and how long does that take? Record the answers in the change description.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Change management, release engineering and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Small batches\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Feature flags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Canary and progressive delivery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database and configuration changes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Change policy and the error budget\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Peer review and risk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Small batches to Feature flags; 2, Feature flags to Canary and progressive delivery; 3, Canary and progressive delivery to Database and configuration changes; 4, Database and configuration changes to Change policy and the error budget; 5, Change policy and the error budget to Peer review and risk.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Small batches \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A release of one change has one suspect when something breaks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large, infrequent releases bundling many changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Feature flags \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A feature flag separates deploying code from releasing a feature.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Feature flags with no owner or expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Canary and progressive delivery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version and compares it with the stable version before continuing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Canaries judged by gut feeling instead of metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Database and configuration changes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Schema changes are the hardest to roll back.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Configuration changes that bypass all release controls.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Change policy and the error budget \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tie release behaviour to your reliability data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Peer review and risk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Review proportionate to risk. A copy change needs less ceremony than a database migration.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Irreversible database migrations coupled to code deploys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-friday-afternoon-config-change\">Incident: the friday afternoon config change\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 16:40 on Friday an engineer edits a timeout value in a shared configuration service for &quot;a quick fix&quot;. It applies to every service at once. Within minutes, requests to the payment provider time out across the platform, and nobody can immediately say what changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>No change record, no gradual rollout, no quick way to see recent changes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -10\nkubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop\ncurl -s https://config.example.com/api/audit?since=30m | jq -r '.[] | [.time,.user,.key,.old,.new] | @tsv'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s https://config.example.com/api/audit?since=30m | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.[] | [.time,.user,.key,.old,.new] | @tsv&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The audit trail reveals the edit. Revert the value, which restores service, and then add safeguards: configuration changes go through the same review, canary and rollback tooling as code; recent changes are shown on every dashboard as annotations; and risky changes avoid Friday afternoons unless they are fixing an incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Large, infrequent releases bundling many changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Feature flags with no owner or expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canaries judged by gut feeling instead of metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configuration changes that bypass all release controls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Irreversible database migrations coupled to code deploys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"small-batches","text":"Small batches"},{"id":"feature-flags","text":"Feature flags"},{"id":"canary-and-progressive-delivery","text":"Canary and progressive delivery"},{"id":"database-and-configuration-changes","text":"Database and configuration changes"},{"id":"change-policy-and-the-error-budget","text":"Change policy and the error budget"},{"id":"peer-review-and-risk","text":"Peer review and risk"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-friday-afternoon-config-change","text":"Incident: the friday afternoon config change"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SRE book: release engineering","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering/"},{"t":"DORA research and metrics","u":"https://dora.dev/"},{"t":"OpenFeature documentation","u":"https://openfeature.dev/docs/reference/intro"}],"text":"Most incidents begin with a change: a deployment, a configuration edit, a new dependency version, a migration. The goal of release engineering is not to stop change, because change is how a business improves, but to make each change small, observable and reversible. What you will learn Why small, frequent changes are safer than rare large ones Feature flags and dark launches Canary releases with automated analysis and rollback Change windows, freezes and tying releases to the error budget Small batches A release of one change has one suspect when something breaks. A release of two hundred changes has two hundred. Frequent small releases make failures easy to locate and easy to revert. This is why teams with strong delivery practices both deploy more often and have lower failure rates. Track four delivery measures (the DORA metrics): deployment frequency , lead time for changes , change failure rate and time to restore service . Improving the first two should not worsen the last two, and if it does, the safety net needs work. Feature flags A feature flag separates deploying code from releasing a feature. Code ships turned off, then is enabled gradually for a team, a percentage of users or a region, and can be switched off instantly without a deployment. def checkout ( cart, user ): if flags.enabled( \"new-payment-flow\" , user=user. id , default= False ): return new_payment_flow(cart) return legacy_payment_flow(cart) flags: new-payment-flow: enabled: true rollout: percentage: 5 allow: [ \"internal-staff\" ] owner: payments-team expires: 2025-09-30 Flags have costs: each one doubles the code paths to test. Give every flag an owner and an expiry date, and delete flags promptly after full rollout. Make sure the default is the safe behaviour when the flag service is unreachable. Canary and progressive delivery A canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version and compares it with the stable version before continuing. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Rollout metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: replicas: 10 selector: matchLabels: app: shop-api strategy: canary: steps: - setWeight: 5 - pause: { duration: 5m } - analysis: templates: - templateName: error-rate-and-latency - setWeight: 25 - pause: { duration: 10m } - setWeight: 50 - pause: { duration: 10m } template: metadata: labels: app: shop-api spec: containers: - name: api image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.3 An analysis step queries your metrics, for example \"5xx rate of the canary is not more than 1.2 times the baseline and p95 latency is under 400 ms\". If the check fails, the rollout aborts and reverts automatically . This depends entirely on trustworthy metrics, so build the SLO dashboards first. $ kubectl argo rollouts get rollout shop-api -n shop --watch $ kubectl argo rollouts abort shop-api -n shop $ kubectl argo rollouts undo shop-api -n shop Database and configuration changes Schema changes are the hardest to roll back. Use expand and contract : add new columns or tables first (compatible with old code), deploy the code that uses them, migrate the data, and only later remove the old structure. Never ship a migration and the code requiring it as one irreversible step. Treat configuration as code: reviewed, versioned, validated, and rolled out through the same pipeline and canary process. Change policy and the error budget Tie release behaviour to your reliability data: While the SLO is met, ship freely When the error budget is nearly spent, slow down, reduce risk and prioritise reliability work When it is exhausted, freeze non-essential changes until it recovers A freeze at peak business times (a sales event, month-end) is reasonable, but a freeze is a blunt tool. Prefer to make releases safer over making them rarer. Peer review and risk Review proportionate to risk. A copy change needs less ceremony than a database migration. Ask of every risky change: What is the blast radius? How will we know it is wrong? How do we undo it, and how long does t"},"sre/production-readiness-reviews":{"html":"\u003cp>Many incidents are not surprises. They come from services that launched without alerts, without a rollback plan, without anyone who knew how to restart them, or without ever being load tested. A production readiness review (PRR) is a structured conversation that looks for those gaps before users find them. Done well, it helps teams; done badly, it becomes a hated form.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a PRR is and when to hold one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A practical checklist by area\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to run the review so it helps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Launch criteria and follow-up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping a service ready after launch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when\">When\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hold a review before a new service takes production traffic, before a big change in traffic or architecture, and when ownership moves between teams. Start early, since findings are cheaper to fix when the design is still flexible. Keep it proportional: a small internal tool needs a light check; a payment service needs a thorough one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checklist-by-area\">Checklist by area\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Ownership and support\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A named owning team and an on-call rotation with an escalation path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A service catalogue entry with contacts, repository, dashboards and runbooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Agreed support hours and severity definitions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Reliability targets\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>SLIs and SLOs written down, with an error budget policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Known dependencies, and what happens when each one fails (timeouts, fallbacks, circuit breakers)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Monitoring and alerting\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Dashboards with traffic, errors, latency and saturation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts on symptoms users feel, each with a runbook link\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs and traces with request ids\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Capacity and performance\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Load test results against expected peak plus headroom\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autoscaling tested; limits and quotas checked\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Behaviour under overload: shed load or degrade, not collapse\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Change and release\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Automated build and deploy, with canary or staged rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rollback tested and fast; database changes backwards compatible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Feature flags for risky behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Failure and recovery\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Backups exist and a restore has been tried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Single points of failure listed with decisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Behaviour on zone loss known; recovery steps documented\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Security and data\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Least-privilege access, secrets in a manager, dependencies scanned\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data classification, retention and privacy requirements met\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit logs for sensitive actions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Documentation\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Architecture diagram, runbooks for the top failure modes, and a launch plan with a rollback trigger\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-the-review\">Running the review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make it a conversation of an hour or two with the team and one or two experienced reviewers from outside, using the checklist as prompts. Ask &quot;what happens when X fails?&quot; rather than &quot;do you have X?&quot;. Record findings with an owner and severity: must fix before launch, should fix within a month, or accepted risk (with the name of who accepted it). Share the checklist ahead of time so the team can prepare, and treat the review as help, not an audit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"after-launch\">After launch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Readiness decays. Re-review yearly, after major incidents, and when the service changes a lot. Use postmortem actions to update the checklist: every incident that a review could have found is a missing question.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A team says &quot;we have monitoring&quot;. What question reveals whether it is useful?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> &quot;Which alert would fire first if the database became slow, and what would the on-call person do?&quot;\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scenario questions test the whole chain from detection to action.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Production readiness reviews and launch checklists fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Checklist by area\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running the review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">After launch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, When to Checklist by area; 2, Checklist by area to Running the review; 3, Running the review to After launch.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>When \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Hold a review before a new service takes production traffic, before a big change in traffic or architecture, and when ownership moves between teams.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating the review as paperwork.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Checklist by area \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ownership and support.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reviewing too late to change anything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Running the review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Make it a conversation of an hour or two with the team and one or two experienced reviewers from outside, using the checklist as prompts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner for follow-up items.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>After launch \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Readiness decays. Re-review yearly, after major incidents, and when the service changes a lot.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never revisiting after launch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-launch-that-nobody-could-roll-back\">Incident: the launch that nobody could roll back\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new recommendation service launches on a Friday and doubles page latency. The team cannot roll back because the database migration removed a column the old version needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./deploy rollback recs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./deploy rollback recs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: migration 0042 is not reversible\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No review had asked how to roll back. The team restored the column from a backup, which took five hours. Afterwards they added &quot;rollback tested&quot; and &quot;migrations compatible&quot; to the checklist, required a launch plan with a rollback trigger, and stopped Friday launches without a reviewed plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating the review as paperwork\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviewing too late to change anything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for follow-up items\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never revisiting after launch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"when","text":"When"},{"id":"checklist-by-area","text":"Checklist by area"},{"id":"running-the-review","text":"Running the review"},{"id":"after-launch","text":"After launch"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-launch-that-nobody-could-roll-back","text":"Incident: the launch that nobody could roll back"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Launch coordination checklist","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/launch-checklist/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Production readiness","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/table-of-contents/"}],"text":"Many incidents are not surprises. They come from services that launched without alerts, without a rollback plan, without anyone who knew how to restart them, or without ever being load tested. A production readiness review (PRR) is a structured conversation that looks for those gaps before users find them. Done well, it helps teams; done badly, it becomes a hated form. What you will learn What a PRR is and when to hold one A practical checklist by area How to run the review so it helps Launch criteria and follow-up Keeping a service ready after launch When Hold a review before a new service takes production traffic, before a big change in traffic or architecture, and when ownership moves between teams. Start early, since findings are cheaper to fix when the design is still flexible. Keep it proportional: a small internal tool needs a light check; a payment service needs a thorough one. Checklist by area Ownership and support A named owning team and an on-call rotation with an escalation path A service catalogue entry with contacts, repository, dashboards and runbooks Agreed support hours and severity definitions Reliability targets SLIs and SLOs written down, with an error budget policy Known dependencies, and what happens when each one fails (timeouts, fallbacks, circuit breakers) Monitoring and alerting Dashboards with traffic, errors, latency and saturation Alerts on symptoms users feel, each with a runbook link Logs and traces with request ids Capacity and performance Load test results against expected peak plus headroom Autoscaling tested; limits and quotas checked Behaviour under overload: shed load or degrade, not collapse Change and release Automated build and deploy, with canary or staged rollout Rollback tested and fast; database changes backwards compatible Feature flags for risky behaviour Failure and recovery Backups exist and a restore has been tried Single points of failure listed with decisions Behaviour on zone loss known; recovery steps documented Security and data Least-privilege access, secrets in a manager, dependencies scanned Data classification, retention and privacy requirements met Audit logs for sensitive actions Documentation Architecture diagram, runbooks for the top failure modes, and a launch plan with a rollback trigger Running the review Make it a conversation of an hour or two with the team and one or two experienced reviewers from outside, using the checklist as prompts. Ask \"what happens when X fails?\" rather than \"do you have X?\". Record findings with an owner and severity: must fix before launch, should fix within a month, or accepted risk (with the name of who accepted it). Share the checklist ahead of time so the team can prepare, and treat the review as help, not an audit. After launch Readiness decays. Re-review yearly, after major incidents, and when the service changes a lot. Use postmortem actions to update the checklist: every incident that a review could have found is a missing question. Predict first. A team says \"we have monitoring\". What question reveals whether it is useful? Your guess Answer: \"Which alert would fire first if the database became slow, and what would the on-call person do?\" Scenario questions test the whole chain from detection to action. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Production readiness reviews and launch checklists fits together Flow in order: 1, When to Checklist by area; 2, Checklist by area to Running the review; 3, Running the review to After launch. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. When Step What it does: Hold a review before a new service takes production traffic, before a big change in traffic or architecture, and when ownership moves between teams. If it fails or is weak: Treating the re"},"sre/incident-communication-and-stakeholders":{"html":"\u003cp>During an incident, the technical fix is only half of the job. Customers want to know whether they are affected, executives want to know the impact, other teams need to know whether to act, and responders need quiet. Poor communication prolongs outages, damages trust and burns out the people fixing the problem. Good communication is a skill you can practise like any other.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Severity levels and what each triggers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separating the response channel from the updates channel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The shape of a good status update\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Customer-facing messages and status pages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handovers, long incidents and the aftermath\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"severity-levels\">Severity levels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define levels by impact, not by cause, and say what each level triggers (who is paged, who must join, how often updates go out).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example impact\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Response\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Sev 1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Core service down for many users, data loss, security breach\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Page everyone needed; commander assigned; updates every 30 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Sev 2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Major feature degraded for some users\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>On-call plus owners; updates every hour\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Sev 3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minor issue with a workaround\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Normal working hours; ticket\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone may raise severity; lowering it should be a decision of the incident commander. If unsure, start high and reduce.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"channels\">Channels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a dedicated incident channel for responders, with a pinned summary: what is happening, who is commander, where the dashboard and the document are. Keep side conversations out. A separate updates channel (or a mailing list) carries the periodic summaries for everyone else, so executives and support do not interrupt responders to ask for news. The communications lead owns that channel and the status page, so the commander and the engineers can concentrate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-good-status-update\">A good status update\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Short, factual and on a schedule, even when there is nothing new (&quot;no change, next update at 15:30&quot;). Include:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Impact:\u003c/strong> who is affected and how, in plain words.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Status:\u003c/strong> what we know and what we are doing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Next update:\u003c/strong> a time.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cblockquote>\u003cp>14:20 UTC: Some customers cannot complete payments (about 30 percent of attempts fail). We have identified a faulty change released at 13:50 and are rolling it back. Next update at 14:50 or sooner.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/blockquote>\n\u003cp>Avoid guesses about the cause, internal jargon, blame, and promises about when it will be fixed unless you are sure. Do not paste raw logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"customers\">Customers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Post on the status page early, within minutes of confirming impact. Say what customers can do (a workaround, retry later). When resolved, say so and what happens next. For serious incidents, follow with a plain account of what happened and what is being changed. If legal or contract rules apply, for example data breach notification, involve the right people early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"long-incidents-and-handover\">Long incidents and handover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a few hours, people make mistakes. Rotate roles, hand over with a written summary (state, hypotheses tried, pending actions), and let people rest. The commander should step back from debugging, and a fresh commander can take over with a clean view.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"afterwards\">Afterwards\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send a final summary, thank those involved, schedule the postmortem within a few days while memory is fresh, and track actions to completion. People who handled an incident well in difficult conditions deserve to hear it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An executive asks in the responders' channel for an estimate of the fix time. What should the commander do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Point them to the updates channel, give them a short answer through the communications lead, and keep the response channel for responders.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Protect the responders' attention while keeping stakeholders informed on a schedule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Incident communication and stakeholder management fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Severity levels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Channels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A good status update\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Customers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Long incidents and handover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Afterwards\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Severity levels to Channels; 2, Channels to A good status update; 3, A good status update to Customers; 4, Customers to Long incidents and handover; 5, Long incidents and handover to Afterwards.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Severity levels \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Define levels by impact, not by cause, and say what each level triggers (who is paged, who must join, how often updates go out).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Everyone talking in the response channel.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Channels \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use a dedicated incident channel for responders, with a pinned summary: what is happening, who is commander, where the dashboard and the document are.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A separate updates channel (or a mailing list) carries the periodic summaries for everyone else, so executives and support do not interrupt...\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>A good status update \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Short, factual and on a schedule, even when there is nothing new (&quot;no change, next update at 15:30&quot;).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No update schedule.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Customers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Post on the status page early, within minutes of confirming impact.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No handover in long incidents.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Long incidents and handover \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After a few hours, people make mistakes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Afterwards \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send a final summary, thank those involved, schedule the postmortem within a few days while memory is fresh, and track actions to completion.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-silent-outage\">Incident: the silent outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A checkout bug affects customers for 90 minutes. Engineers fix it quickly, but support learns about it from angry social media posts.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">14:05 detected   14:12 fix started   15:35 resolved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">status page: first update at 15:50\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had no communications role, the status page needed approval from someone who was asleep, and nobody told support. Afterwards they named a communications lead in the on-call rota, allowed the on-call engineer to post an initial status message, created templates for first updates, and added support to the updates channel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No update schedule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Speculating about the cause or blaming a person\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Everyone talking in the response channel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No handover in long incidents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"severity-levels","text":"Severity levels"},{"id":"channels","text":"Channels"},{"id":"a-good-status-update","text":"A good status update"},{"id":"customers","text":"Customers"},{"id":"long-incidents-and-handover","text":"Long incidents and handover"},{"id":"afterwards","text":"Afterwards"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-silent-outage","text":"Incident: the silent outage"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Managing Incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"},{"t":"Atlassian: Incident management handbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/incident-management"}],"text":"During an incident, the technical fix is only half of the job. Customers want to know whether they are affected, executives want to know the impact, other teams need to know whether to act, and responders need quiet. Poor communication prolongs outages, damages trust and burns out the people fixing the problem. Good communication is a skill you can practise like any other. What you will learn Severity levels and what each triggers Separating the response channel from the updates channel The shape of a good status update Customer-facing messages and status pages Handovers, long incidents and the aftermath Severity levels Define levels by impact, not by cause, and say what each level triggers (who is paged, who must join, how often updates go out). Level Example impact Response Sev 1 Core service down for many users, data loss, security breach Page everyone needed; commander assigned; updates every 30 minutes Sev 2 Major feature degraded for some users On-call plus owners; updates every hour Sev 3 Minor issue with a workaround Normal working hours; ticket Anyone may raise severity; lowering it should be a decision of the incident commander. If unsure, start high and reduce. Channels Use a dedicated incident channel for responders, with a pinned summary: what is happening, who is commander, where the dashboard and the document are. Keep side conversations out. A separate updates channel (or a mailing list) carries the periodic summaries for everyone else, so executives and support do not interrupt responders to ask for news. The communications lead owns that channel and the status page, so the commander and the engineers can concentrate. A good status update Short, factual and on a schedule, even when there is nothing new (\"no change, next update at 15:30\"). Include: Impact: who is affected and how, in plain words. Status: what we know and what we are doing. Next update: a time. 14:20 UTC: Some customers cannot complete payments (about 30 percent of attempts fail). We have identified a faulty change released at 13:50 and are rolling it back. Next update at 14:50 or sooner. Avoid guesses about the cause, internal jargon, blame, and promises about when it will be fixed unless you are sure. Do not paste raw logs. Customers Post on the status page early, within minutes of confirming impact. Say what customers can do (a workaround, retry later). When resolved, say so and what happens next. For serious incidents, follow with a plain account of what happened and what is being changed. If legal or contract rules apply, for example data breach notification, involve the right people early. Long incidents and handover After a few hours, people make mistakes. Rotate roles, hand over with a written summary (state, hypotheses tried, pending actions), and let people rest. The commander should step back from debugging, and a fresh commander can take over with a clean view. Afterwards Send a final summary, thank those involved, schedule the postmortem within a few days while memory is fresh, and track actions to completion. People who handled an incident well in difficult conditions deserve to hear it. Predict first. An executive asks in the responders' channel for an estimate of the fix time. What should the commander do? Your guess Answer: Point them to the updates channel, give them a short answer through the communications lead, and keep the response channel for responders. Protect the responders' attention while keeping stakeholders informed on a schedule. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Incident communication and stakeholder management fits together Flow in order: 1, Severity levels to Channels; 2, Channels to A good status update; 3, A good status update to Customers; 4, Customers to Long incidents and handover; 5, Long incidents and handover to Afterwards. Step Numbered arrow"},"sre/dependencies-and-third-party-risk":{"html":"\u003cp>Your service has a 99.95 percent target. It calls a payment provider with a 99.9 percent target, a search service at 99.9 percent and a messaging service at 99.5 percent. Your target is higher than some of your own dependencies. Reliability maths, and plain honesty about dependencies, change what promises you can make.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How dependencies limit the availability you can offer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Map hard and soft dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mitigations: timeouts, fallbacks, caching, queues and redundancy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Managing third-party providers: contracts, status, and exit plans\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-arithmetic-of-dependencies\">The arithmetic of dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a request needs services A and B, and both must work, the availability is the product: 99.9% x 99.9% = 99.8%. With five such dependencies at 99.9 percent, you cannot offer better than about 99.5 percent, however good your own code is. This is why every hard dependency must be visible in your target: either lower the promise, reduce the number of hard dependencies, or add a way to work when one fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Dependencies in the request path\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Each at 99.9%\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Combined\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9%\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.7%\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9%\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.5%\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"draw-the-dependency-map\">Draw the dependency map\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List what a request needs: databases, caches, internal services, cloud services, DNS, identity providers, payment and email vendors. Mark each as \u003cstrong>hard\u003c/strong> (the request fails without it) or \u003cstrong>soft\u003c/strong> (the request still gives a useful answer). For each, note the owner, the target they publish, and how you would notice a failure. Include the hidden ones: DNS, certificates, time sync, the CI system you need to deploy a fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-soft-what-you-can\">Make soft what you can\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Turn hard dependencies into soft ones where the product allows it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Timeouts and budgets\u003c/strong>: a call must fail quickly so that it does not use up your own capacity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fallbacks\u003c/strong>: show cached prices, or hide recommendations, when the recommender is down.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Queues\u003c/strong>: accept an order now and send the confirmation email later.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Circuit breakers\u003c/strong>: stop calling a failing dependency for a short time, then probe again.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Redundancy\u003c/strong>: a second provider for critical functions such as payments or email, with a tested switch.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Each fallback must be tested, because an untested fallback usually fails during the first real outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"third-party-providers\">Third-party providers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For external vendors, read the SLA: what is measured, what credit you get, and what is excluded. A credit is not compensation for your lost customers. Subscribe to the provider status page and alert on it, but also measure the dependency yourself, because status pages lag. Keep an exit plan for critical vendors: data export, a tested alternative, and a decision on how long an outage you would tolerate before switching. Review dependency health in your regular reliability review.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your service needs four hard dependencies, each at 99.9 percent. What is the best availability you can honestly promise?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> About 99.6 percent (0.999 to the power of four), before your own failures.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Availabilities multiply when all must work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Dependencies and third-party risk fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The arithmetic of dependencies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Draw the dependency map\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Make soft what you can\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Third-party providers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The arithmetic of dependencies to Draw the dependency map; 2, Draw the dependency map to Make soft what you can; 3, Make soft what you can to Third-party providers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The arithmetic of dependencies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If a request needs services A and B, and both must work, the availability is the product: 99.9% x 99.9% = 99.8%.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Promising more than the product of your hard dependencies.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Draw the dependency map \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> List what a request needs: databases, caches, internal services, cloud services, DNS, identity providers, payment and email vendors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgetting hidden dependencies such as DNS and identity.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Make soft what you can \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Turn hard dependencies into soft ones where the product allows it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fallbacks that were never tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Third-party providers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> For external vendors, read the SLA: what is measured, what credit you get, and what is excluded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Trusting a vendor status page alone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-our-99-95-depended-on-a-99-5\">Incident: Our 99.95 depended on a 99.5\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A product promised customers 99.95 percent availability, then missed it every quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./dependency-report.sh --quarter Q4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./dependency-report.sh --quarter Q4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">service        role   target   measured\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments-api   hard   99.9     99.82\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search         soft   99.9     99.71\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">mail-gateway   hard   99.5     99.40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sms-vendor     hard   99.5     99.20\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Three hard dependencies had lower targets than the promise. The review led to three changes: the email and SMS calls moved to a queue, so they became soft; search results fell back to a cached list; and the public target changed to 99.9 percent for the checkout path. The team measured each dependency from their own side, not only from provider status pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Promising more than the product of your hard dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting hidden dependencies such as DNS and identity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fallbacks that were never tested\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting a vendor status page alone\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-arithmetic-of-dependencies","text":"The arithmetic of dependencies"},{"id":"draw-the-dependency-map","text":"Draw the dependency map"},{"id":"make-soft-what-you-can","text":"Make soft what you can"},{"id":"third-party-providers","text":"Third-party providers"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-our-99-95-depended-on-a-99-5","text":"Incident: Our 99.95 depended on a 99.5"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Embracing risk","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: Implementing SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},{"t":"SRE book: service level objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"}],"text":"Your service has a 99.95 percent target. It calls a payment provider with a 99.9 percent target, a search service at 99.9 percent and a messaging service at 99.5 percent. Your target is higher than some of your own dependencies. Reliability maths, and plain honesty about dependencies, change what promises you can make. What you will learn How dependencies limit the availability you can offer Map hard and soft dependencies Mitigations: timeouts, fallbacks, caching, queues and redundancy Managing third-party providers: contracts, status, and exit plans The arithmetic of dependencies If a request needs services A and B, and both must work, the availability is the product: 99.9% x 99.9% = 99.8%. With five such dependencies at 99.9 percent, you cannot offer better than about 99.5 percent, however good your own code is. This is why every hard dependency must be visible in your target: either lower the promise, reduce the number of hard dependencies, or add a way to work when one fails. Dependencies in the request path Each at 99.9% Combined 1 99.9% 99.9% 3 99.9% 99.7% 5 99.9% 99.5% Draw the dependency map List what a request needs: databases, caches, internal services, cloud services, DNS, identity providers, payment and email vendors. Mark each as hard (the request fails without it) or soft (the request still gives a useful answer). For each, note the owner, the target they publish, and how you would notice a failure. Include the hidden ones: DNS, certificates, time sync, the CI system you need to deploy a fix. Make soft what you can Turn hard dependencies into soft ones where the product allows it. Timeouts and budgets : a call must fail quickly so that it does not use up your own capacity. Fallbacks : show cached prices, or hide recommendations, when the recommender is down. Queues : accept an order now and send the confirmation email later. Circuit breakers : stop calling a failing dependency for a short time, then probe again. Redundancy : a second provider for critical functions such as payments or email, with a tested switch. Each fallback must be tested, because an untested fallback usually fails during the first real outage. Third-party providers For external vendors, read the SLA: what is measured, what credit you get, and what is excluded. A credit is not compensation for your lost customers. Subscribe to the provider status page and alert on it, but also measure the dependency yourself, because status pages lag. Keep an exit plan for critical vendors: data export, a tested alternative, and a decision on how long an outage you would tolerate before switching. Review dependency health in your regular reliability review. Predict first. Your service needs four hard dependencies, each at 99.9 percent. What is the best availability you can honestly promise? Your guess Answer: About 99.6 percent (0.999 to the power of four), before your own failures. Availabilities multiply when all must work. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Dependencies and third-party risk fits together Flow in order: 1, The arithmetic of dependencies to Draw the dependency map; 2, Draw the dependency map to Make soft what you can; 3, Make soft what you can to Third-party providers. Step Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The arithmetic of dependencies Step What it does: If a request needs services A and B, and both must work, the availability is the product: 99.9% x 99.9% = 99.8%. If it fails or is weak: Promising more than the product of your hard dependencies. Draw the dependency map Step What it does: List what a request needs: databases, caches, internal services, cloud services, DNS, identity providers, payment and email vendors. If it fails or is weak: Forgetting hidden dependencies such as DNS and"},"sre/disaster-recovery-exercises":{"html":"\u003cp>The recovery plan says &quot;restore from backup in the secondary region&quot;. Nobody has done it, the credentials for the secondary account live on a laptop, and the backup tool changed its version last year. A plan that has not been exercised is a hope. Drills turn it into a capability and show the gaps while the stakes are low.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Recovery objectives: RTO, RPO and what they cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Types of exercises: tabletop, partial, and full failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run a drill safely, with roles and a stop rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turn findings into fixes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objectives-first\">Objectives first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured in time. A payment system may need an RTO of 15 minutes and an RPO of near zero, which needs replication and an automated failover. An internal report may accept an RTO of a day and an RPO of 24 hours, which a nightly backup can meet. Agree on objectives with the business per service, and write down what each one costs, because lower numbers mean more infrastructure and more practice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kinds-of-exercises\">Kinds of exercises\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start small and grow:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Tabletop\u003c/strong>: a group talks through a scenario (&quot;the primary region is gone&quot;) and follows the plan on paper. It finds missing steps, contacts and decisions cheaply.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Component restore\u003c/strong>: restore a database from backup into a clean environment, and check the data and the time taken.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Partial failover\u003c/strong>: move one non-critical service to the secondary region during working hours.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Full failover\u003c/strong>: switch real production traffic. Do this only when earlier steps are boring.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Run them regularly (for example once or twice a year for the important systems), and after big changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-it-safely\">Run it safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Treat a drill like a change. Define the scenario and the success criteria (for example RTO 30 minutes, RPO 5 minutes), name a lead, a scribe and observers, and write down a \u003cstrong>stop rule\u003c/strong>: conditions under which you abort and go back. Announce the window to support and partners. During the drill, record timestamps for each step: detection, decision, start of recovery, first successful request, full service. Do not help the team with knowledge that a real responder would not have, or you will hide the gaps you came to find.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"learn-and-fix\">Learn and fix\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The most valuable output is a list of surprises: an expired credential, a missing DNS record, a runbook step that needs a person who is on holiday, a restore that took four hours not one. Write them up blamelessly, give each one an owner and a date, and repeat the exercise to prove the fixes. Track measured recovery time and data loss against the objectives over time. If you cannot meet an objective, either invest to improve the system or change the objective openly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A restore drill shows an RTO of 4 hours against a target of 1 hour. What are your options?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Improve the process or the design (automation, replication, faster storage) until it meets the target, or agree a new target with the business. Do not leave the gap undocumented.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Objectives must match what you can actually do.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Disaster recovery exercises fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Objectives first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kinds of exercises\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Run it safely\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Learn and fix\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Objectives first to Kinds of exercises; 2, Kinds of exercises to Run it safely; 3, Run it safely to Learn and fix.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Objectives first \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RTO (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Kinds of exercises \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start small and grow.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never exercising the plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Run it safely \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Treat a drill like a change. Define the scenario and the success criteria (for example RTO 30 minutes, RPO 5 minutes), name a lead, a scribe and observers, and write...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Helping the team during the drill so gaps stay hidden.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Learn and fix \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The most valuable output is a list of surprises: an expired credential, a missing DNS record, a runbook step that needs a person who is on holiday, a restore that took...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Success criteria that are not measurable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restore-that-took-two-days\">Incident: The restore that took two days\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a storage failure in a test environment, restoring the main database from backup took two days instead of the planned two hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"time pgbackrest --stanza=main restore\npgbackrest info | grep &quot;database size&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> pgbackrest --stanza=main restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real    1612m41s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest info | grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database size&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database size: 4.1TB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The plan had been written when the database was 300 GB. It had grown to 4.1 TB, and the restore went through a single slow link. The team added regional restore targets with faster disks, switched to incremental backups with delta restore, and introduced a quarterly timed restore with an alert if it grows beyond 80 percent of the RTO. They also moved the credentials for the secondary account into the secret manager that works during a primary region outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never exercising the plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Success criteria that are not measurable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Helping the team during the drill so gaps stay hidden\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not fixing the findings, or not repeating the test\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"objectives-first","text":"Objectives first"},{"id":"kinds-of-exercises","text":"Kinds of exercises"},{"id":"run-it-safely","text":"Run it safely"},{"id":"learn-and-fix","text":"Learn and fix"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-restore-that-took-two-days","text":"Incident: The restore that took two days"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Data integrity","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/data-integrity/"},{"t":"NIST SP 800-34 Contingency planning guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/upd1/final"}],"text":"The recovery plan says \"restore from backup in the secondary region\". Nobody has done it, the credentials for the secondary account live on a laptop, and the backup tool changed its version last year. A plan that has not been exercised is a hope. Drills turn it into a capability and show the gaps while the stakes are low. What you will learn Recovery objectives: RTO, RPO and what they cost Types of exercises: tabletop, partial, and full failover Run a drill safely, with roles and a stop rule Turn findings into fixes Objectives first RTO (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured in time. A payment system may need an RTO of 15 minutes and an RPO of near zero, which needs replication and an automated failover. An internal report may accept an RTO of a day and an RPO of 24 hours, which a nightly backup can meet. Agree on objectives with the business per service, and write down what each one costs, because lower numbers mean more infrastructure and more practice. Kinds of exercises Start small and grow: Tabletop : a group talks through a scenario (\"the primary region is gone\") and follows the plan on paper. It finds missing steps, contacts and decisions cheaply. Component restore : restore a database from backup into a clean environment, and check the data and the time taken. Partial failover : move one non-critical service to the secondary region during working hours. Full failover : switch real production traffic. Do this only when earlier steps are boring. Run them regularly (for example once or twice a year for the important systems), and after big changes. Run it safely Treat a drill like a change. Define the scenario and the success criteria (for example RTO 30 minutes, RPO 5 minutes), name a lead, a scribe and observers, and write down a stop rule : conditions under which you abort and go back. Announce the window to support and partners. During the drill, record timestamps for each step: detection, decision, start of recovery, first successful request, full service. Do not help the team with knowledge that a real responder would not have, or you will hide the gaps you came to find. Learn and fix The most valuable output is a list of surprises: an expired credential, a missing DNS record, a runbook step that needs a person who is on holiday, a restore that took four hours not one. Write them up blamelessly, give each one an owner and a date, and repeat the exercise to prove the fixes. Track measured recovery time and data loss against the objectives over time. If you cannot meet an objective, either invest to improve the system or change the objective openly. Predict first. A restore drill shows an RTO of 4 hours against a target of 1 hour. What are your options? Your guess Answer: Improve the process or the design (automation, replication, faster storage) until it meets the target, or agree a new target with the business. Do not leave the gap undocumented. Objectives must match what you can actually do. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Disaster recovery exercises fits together Flow in order: 1, Objectives first to Kinds of exercises; 2, Kinds of exercises to Run it safely; 3, Run it safely to Learn and fix. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Objectives first Step What it does: RTO (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Kinds of exercises Step What it does: Start small and grow. If it fails or is weak: Never exercising the plan. Run it safely Step What it does: Treat a drill like a change. Define the scenario and the success criteria (for example RTO 30 minutes, RPO 5 minutes)"},"sre/debugging-under-pressure":{"html":"\u003cp>Debugging in production differs from debugging on a laptop. Users are affected now, you cannot pause the system, and several people are looking at it at once. The goal changes: first stop the harm, then understand the cause. A calm method keeps a team from making things worse.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mitigate first, investigate second\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Working with hypotheses and one change at a time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles in an incident and what to write down\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading recent changes as the first suspect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Closing the loop: verification and follow-up\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mitigate-then-understand\">Mitigate, then understand\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The quickest safe action usually is not the deepest fix: roll back the last deploy, turn off a feature flag, shift traffic away from a bad zone, or add capacity. Do this first if it is safe and reversible, and then investigate while users are no longer harmed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hypothesis-loop\">Hypothesis loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>State what you see (symptom, start time, affected users).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>List what changed near the start: deploys, flags, config, traffic, certificates, dependencies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pick the most likely and cheapest hypothesis to test.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test it with a query or a read-only command.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Change one thing, and watch the same metric.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Write each hypothesis and the result in the incident channel. It prevents repeated work and builds the timeline for the review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Incident commander\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Decides, assigns work, keeps the pace; does not debug\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Operations lead\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs the commands and changes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Communications lead\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Updates stakeholders and the status page on a schedule\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Scribe\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keeps a timestamped log of actions and findings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On a small team one person holds two roles, but the commander should not be the one with hands on the keyboard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-questions\">Useful questions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What changed? What is different between affected and unaffected users?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is it getting worse, steady or improving?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is the blast radius, and can we reduce it?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What would we expect to see if this hypothesis were true? Do we see it?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Compare affected and unaffected quickly\nkubectl rollout history deploy/shop\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20\ncurl -s &quot;$STATUS_URL/api/v1/changes?since=2h&quot; | jq '.[].summary'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Compare affected and unaffected quickly\u003c/span>\nkubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deploy/shop\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -20\ncurl -s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$STATUS_URL\u003c/span>/api/v1/changes?since=2h&quot;\u003c/span> | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.[].summary&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Error rate rose at 10:02, and a deploy finished at 10:00. You are unsure it is the cause. What is the safest first step?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Roll back the deploy if rollback is safe, then check whether the error rate recovers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A rollback is quick, reversible, and doubles as a test of the hypothesis. Investigation continues afterwards from a stable system.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"detect&gt;mit;detect&gt;hyp;mit&gt;verify;hyp&gt;verify;verify&gt;review\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Incident investigation loop\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"detect\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Detect and declare\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mit\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mitigate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hyp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hypothesise and test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"verify\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"review\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blameless review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Detect and declare to Mitigate; 2, Detect and declare to Hypothesise and test; 3, Mitigate to Verify recovery; 4, Hypothesise and test to Verify recovery; 5, Verify recovery to Blameless review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"detect\">\u003cdt>Detect and declare \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An alert or report is confirmed and an incident is declared with a commander.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Delay in declaring means uncoordinated, duplicated effort.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mit\">\u003cdt>Mitigate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Roll back, flag off, fail over or add capacity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Digging for root cause while users are failing prolongs harm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hyp\">\u003cdt>Hypothesise and test \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> List changes, pick the likeliest, test it cheaply.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Several simultaneous changes destroy the evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"verify\">\u003cdt>Verify recovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Confirm user-facing metrics returned to normal.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Declaring victory on internal graphs while users still fail.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"review\">\u003cdt>Blameless review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write the timeline, causes and follow-up actions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping the review repeats the incident.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-people-three-changes\">Incident: three people, three changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Checkout latency triples at 16:20. Three engineers each make a change within five minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">16:24 alice  restarted payments pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">16:26 bob    increased db pool 20 -&gt; 50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">16:28 chen   disabled the new recommendations flag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">16:31 latency back to normal. which change fixed it?\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Latency recovered, but nobody knew why. Bob&#39;s larger pool had also raised database load, and the problem returned the next day. In the review the team agreed on one operator making changes at a time, announced in the channel before each action, and a rule that a rollback comes before experiments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Several people changing things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No written timeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the check that users have recovered\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blame, which makes people hide information\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"mitigate-then-understand","text":"Mitigate, then understand"},{"id":"hypothesis-loop","text":"Hypothesis loop"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"useful-questions","text":"Useful questions"},{"id":"incident-three-people-three-changes","text":"Incident: three people, three changes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Effective Troubleshooting","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/effective-troubleshooting/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: Managing Incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"}],"text":"Debugging in production differs from debugging on a laptop. Users are affected now, you cannot pause the system, and several people are looking at it at once. The goal changes: first stop the harm, then understand the cause. A calm method keeps a team from making things worse. What you will learn Mitigate first, investigate second Working with hypotheses and one change at a time Roles in an incident and what to write down Reading recent changes as the first suspect Closing the loop: verification and follow-up Mitigate, then understand The quickest safe action usually is not the deepest fix: roll back the last deploy, turn off a feature flag, shift traffic away from a bad zone, or add capacity. Do this first if it is safe and reversible, and then investigate while users are no longer harmed. Hypothesis loop State what you see (symptom, start time, affected users). List what changed near the start: deploys, flags, config, traffic, certificates, dependencies. Pick the most likely and cheapest hypothesis to test. Test it with a query or a read-only command. Change one thing, and watch the same metric. Write each hypothesis and the result in the incident channel. It prevents repeated work and builds the timeline for the review. Roles Role Job Incident commander Decides, assigns work, keeps the pace; does not debug Operations lead Runs the commands and changes Communications lead Updates stakeholders and the status page on a schedule Scribe Keeps a timestamped log of actions and findings On a small team one person holds two roles, but the commander should not be the one with hands on the keyboard. Useful questions What changed? What is different between affected and unaffected users? Is it getting worse, steady or improving? What is the blast radius, and can we reduce it? What would we expect to see if this hypothesis were true? Do we see it? # Compare affected and unaffected quickly kubectl rollout history deploy/shop kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20 curl -s \" $STATUS_URL /api/v1/changes?since=2h\" | jq &#x27;.[].summary&#x27; Predict first. Error rate rose at 10:02, and a deploy finished at 10:00. You are unsure it is the cause. What is the safest first step? Your guess Answer: Roll back the deploy if rollback is safe, then check whether the error rate recovers. A rollback is quick, reversible, and doubles as a test of the hypothesis. Investigation continues afterwards from a stable system. Incident investigation loop Flow in order: 1, Detect and declare to Mitigate; 2, Detect and declare to Hypothesise and test; 3, Mitigate to Verify recovery; 4, Hypothesise and test to Verify recovery; 5, Verify recovery to Blameless review. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Detect and declare Step What it does: An alert or report is confirmed and an incident is declared with a commander. If it fails or is weak: Delay in declaring means uncoordinated, duplicated effort. Mitigate Step What it does: Roll back, flag off, fail over or add capacity. If it fails or is weak: Digging for root cause while users are failing prolongs harm. Hypothesise and test Step What it does: List changes, pick the likeliest, test it cheaply. If it fails or is weak: Several simultaneous changes destroy the evidence. Verify recovery Step What it does: Confirm user-facing metrics returned to normal. If it fails or is weak: Declaring victory on internal graphs while users still fail. Blameless review Step What it does: Write the timeline, causes and follow-up actions. If it fails or is weak: Skipping the review repeats the incident. Incident: three people, three changes Incident Checkout latency triples at 16:20. Three engineers each make a change within five minutes. 16:24 alice restarted payments pods 16:26 bob increased db pool 20 -> 50 16:28 chen disabled the new recommendations flag 16:31 latency back to normal. which change fixed it? Latency recovered, but nob"},"sre/senior-skills-writing-design-docs-and-rfcs":{"html":"\u003cp>At a senior level your output is increasingly decisions, not code. A design document is how you make a decision visible, test it against other people&#39;s knowledge and leave a record. A good one is short, specific and honest about what is uncertain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>When a design doc is worth writing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A structure that readers can scan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to present options and trade-offs fairly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running review and recording the decision\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-to-write-one\">When to write one\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a design document when a change is hard to reverse, affects several teams, has security or cost impact, or when you cannot explain the approach in a few sentences. Skip it for small, reversible changes where a pull request description is enough. The cost of a document is a day or two; the cost of a wrong design discovered after launch is months.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-structure-that-works\">A structure that works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"title: &quot;Move session storage to a managed cache&quot;\nstatus: draft            # draft, in review, accepted, rejected, superseded\nauthors: [&quot;A. Rao&quot;]\nreviewers: [&quot;platform&quot;, &quot;security&quot;, &quot;checkout&quot;]\nsections:\n  - context: &quot;Why now. What is broken or missing. One paragraph.&quot;\n  - goals: &quot;What success looks like, with numbers where possible.&quot;\n  - non_goals: &quot;What this design deliberately does not solve.&quot;\n  - proposal: &quot;The chosen design in enough detail to build and review.&quot;\n  - alternatives: &quot;Two or three real options and why they lost.&quot;\n  - risks: &quot;What could go wrong, how we will notice, how we roll back.&quot;\n  - rollout: &quot;Steps, owners, dates, and the decision points.&quot;\n  - open_questions: &quot;What we do not know yet.&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">title:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Move session storage to a managed cache&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">status:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">draft\u003c/span>            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># draft, in review, accepted, rejected, superseded\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">authors:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;A. Rao&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">reviewers:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;security&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">sections:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">context:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Why now. What is broken or missing. One paragraph.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">goals:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What success looks like, with numbers where possible.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">non_goals:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What this design deliberately does not solve.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">proposal:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The chosen design in enough detail to build and review.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">alternatives:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Two or three real options and why they lost.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">risks:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What could go wrong, how we will notice, how we roll back.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rollout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Steps, owners, dates, and the decision points.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">open_questions:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What we do not know yet.&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Start with the \u003cstrong>context and goals\u003c/strong>, because reviewers cannot judge a solution without the problem. State \u003cstrong>non-goals\u003c/strong> to stop scope creep. Put a short summary at the top that a busy executive can read in one minute. Use a diagram for flows and failure paths, and prefer concrete numbers (traffic, data size, latency, cost) over adjectives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"options-and-trade-offs\">Options and trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The most useful section is \u003cstrong>alternatives\u003c/strong>. Present real options, including &quot;do nothing&quot; and a simpler one, with the same criteria for each: effort, risk, cost, operational burden, reversibility. Say why you prefer yours and what would change your mind. A document with one option reads as a decision already made, and reviewers disengage. Be honest about weaknesses of your own proposal; you will be trusted more.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review-and-decision\">Review and decision\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Share early as a draft, ask named people for specific input (&quot;security: does this meet the key-handling rule?&quot;), and set a review deadline. Respond to every comment, even with &quot;not doing this because...&quot;. Hold a short meeting only for unresolved points. When the decision is made, change the status, record the date and who decided, and link the document from the code or repository. An \u003cstrong>architecture decision record\u003c/strong> (a one-page summary per decision) is useful for the long term.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Reviewers keep asking &quot;why not use the managed service?&quot; in a design that did not mention it. What was missing?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The alternatives section. Add the managed option with fair criteria and a clear reason for the choice.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reviewers will supply the options you did not.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: writing design docs and RFCs fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When to write one\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A structure that works\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Options and trade-offs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review and decision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, When to write one to A structure that works; 2, A structure that works to Options and trade-offs; 3, Options and trade-offs to Review and decision.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>When to write one \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write a design document when a change is hard to reverse, affects several teams, has security or cost impact, or when you cannot explain the approach in a few sentences.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Writing a design after the code is done.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A structure that works \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with the context and goals, because reviewers cannot judge a solution without the problem.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Burying the key decision in a long document.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Options and trade-offs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The most useful section is alternatives.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One option, presented as the only one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Review and decision \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Share early as a draft, ask named people for specific input (&quot;security: does this meet the key-handling rule?&quot;), and set a review deadline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No named reviewers and no deadline.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-design-nobody-read\">Incident: The design nobody read\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 30-page design for a new deployment system was circulated on Friday and approved on Monday with two comments, and then a month later the on-call team refused to run it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"wc -w deployment-system-design.md\ngrep -c &quot;rollback&quot; deployment-system-design.md\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -w deployment-system-design.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">11840 deployment-system-design.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rollback&quot;\u003c/span> deployment-system-design.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The document was long, buried the key decision on page 14, had no alternatives section and no named reviewers, so the on-call team never saw it. After launch they found there was no easy rollback. The author rewrote it as a five-page document with a one-page summary, risks and rollback, asked the on-call team and security for explicit review, and the second version found three real issues before any code shipped.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing a design after the code is done\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One option, presented as the only one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Burying the key decision in a long document\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No named reviewers and no deadline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"when-to-write-one","text":"When to write one"},{"id":"a-structure-that-works","text":"A structure that works"},{"id":"options-and-trade-offs","text":"Options and trade-offs"},{"id":"review-and-decision","text":"Review and decision"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-design-nobody-read","text":"Incident: The design nobody read"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Martin Fowler: architecture decision record","u":"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ArchitectureDecisionRecord.html"},{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"},{"t":"Martin Fowler: scaling the practice of architecture, conversationally","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/scaling-architecture-conversationally.html"}],"text":"At a senior level your output is increasingly decisions, not code. A design document is how you make a decision visible, test it against other people&#39;s knowledge and leave a record. A good one is short, specific and honest about what is uncertain. What you will learn When a design doc is worth writing A structure that readers can scan How to present options and trade-offs fairly Running review and recording the decision When to write one Write a design document when a change is hard to reverse, affects several teams, has security or cost impact, or when you cannot explain the approach in a few sentences. Skip it for small, reversible changes where a pull request description is enough. The cost of a document is a day or two; the cost of a wrong design discovered after launch is months. A structure that works title: \"Move session storage to a managed cache\" status: draft # draft, in review, accepted, rejected, superseded authors: [ \"A. Rao\" ] reviewers: [ \"platform\" , \"security\" , \"checkout\" ] sections: - context: \"Why now. What is broken or missing. One paragraph.\" - goals: \"What success looks like, with numbers where possible.\" - non_goals: \"What this design deliberately does not solve.\" - proposal: \"The chosen design in enough detail to build and review.\" - alternatives: \"Two or three real options and why they lost.\" - risks: \"What could go wrong, how we will notice, how we roll back.\" - rollout: \"Steps, owners, dates, and the decision points.\" - open_questions: \"What we do not know yet.\" Start with the context and goals , because reviewers cannot judge a solution without the problem. State non-goals to stop scope creep. Put a short summary at the top that a busy executive can read in one minute. Use a diagram for flows and failure paths, and prefer concrete numbers (traffic, data size, latency, cost) over adjectives. Options and trade-offs The most useful section is alternatives . Present real options, including \"do nothing\" and a simpler one, with the same criteria for each: effort, risk, cost, operational burden, reversibility. Say why you prefer yours and what would change your mind. A document with one option reads as a decision already made, and reviewers disengage. Be honest about weaknesses of your own proposal; you will be trusted more. Review and decision Share early as a draft, ask named people for specific input (\"security: does this meet the key-handling rule?\"), and set a review deadline. Respond to every comment, even with \"not doing this because...\". Hold a short meeting only for unresolved points. When the decision is made, change the status, record the date and who decided, and link the document from the code or repository. An architecture decision record (a one-page summary per decision) is useful for the long term. Predict first. Reviewers keep asking \"why not use the managed service?\" in a design that did not mention it. What was missing? Your guess Answer: The alternatives section. Add the managed option with fair criteria and a clear reason for the choice. Reviewers will supply the options you did not. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: writing design docs and RFCs fits together Flow in order: 1, When to write one to A structure that works; 2, A structure that works to Options and trade-offs; 3, Options and trade-offs to Review and decision. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. When to write one Step What it does: Write a design document when a change is hard to reverse, affects several teams, has security or cost impact, or when you cannot explain the approach in a few sentences. If it fails or is weak: Writing a design after the code is done. A structure that works Step What it does: Start with the context and goals, because reviewers cannot "},"sre/senior-skills-estimating-and-planning-work":{"html":"\u003cp>&quot;How long will it take?&quot; is the question every senior engineer must answer, and every estimate is partly a guess. The aim is not to be exact but to be useful: a range with stated assumptions, updated as you learn, so that others can plan around it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why estimates fail and how to reduce the error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving ranges and confidence instead of single dates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Slicing work and finding the risky parts first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planning for operations, review and the unknown\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-estimates-are-wrong\">Why estimates are wrong\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>People estimate the happy path and forget reviews, testing, deployment, waiting for others, meetings, incidents and the discovery of unknowns. They anchor on the first number they heard, and they feel pressure to give a short one. Work with new technology or unclear requirements is the most uncertain. Past data helps more than intuition: compare with similar tasks you finished and how long they really took.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"give-a-range-and-state-assumptions\">Give a range and state assumptions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Say &quot;two to four weeks, most likely three, assuming the API owner reviews within two days and we reuse the existing auth module&quot;. A range tells the listener the uncertainty. A useful technique is three points: best case, likely and worst case. Convert the range into a commitment only when risk is reduced: &quot;I&#39;ll know more after the spike on Thursday, and then I&#39;ll give a date I&#39;d stake my name on.&quot; Tell people when an assumption breaks, as early as you notice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"estimate:\n  task: &quot;Migrate billing jobs to the new scheduler&quot;\n  range_weeks: [3, 6]\n  most_likely_weeks: 4\n  confidence: medium\n  assumptions:\n    - &quot;Scheduler team supports the retry feature by week 2&quot;\n    - &quot;No change to the invoice format&quot;\n  risks:\n    - &quot;Hidden dependency on cron environment variables&quot;\n  first_step: &quot;Two-day spike to list all jobs and dependencies&quot;\n  next_update: &quot;Friday&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">estimate:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">task:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Migrate billing jobs to the new scheduler&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">range_weeks:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">most_likely_weeks:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">confidence:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">medium\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">assumptions:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Scheduler team supports the retry feature by week 2&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;No change to the invoice format&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">risks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hidden dependency on cron environment variables&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">first_step:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Two-day spike to list all jobs and dependencies&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">next_update:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Friday&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"slice-and-de-risk\">Slice and de-risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Break the work into slices that each deliver something testable, ideally in a few days. Do the riskiest or most uncertain parts first (a spike or prototype), so bad news arrives early. Avoid &quot;90 percent done&quot;: define done as running in production with monitoring and a documented rollback. Include time for code review, tests, documentation, migration and cleanup, and for being on call. For shared work, map dependencies on other teams and ask them for their own dates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-for-the-unknown-and-say-no-clearly\">Plan for the unknown and say no clearly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep slack: a plan fully booked at 100 percent fails at the first surprise. A rough rule is to plan 60 to 70 percent of capacity for committed work, leaving the rest for incidents, support and learning. When asked to shorten a schedule, offer options instead of only refusing: reduce scope, add a phase, move the date, or add help (which has a startup cost). Make trade-offs visible: &quot;We can ship by the 15th without the audit log, or by the 29th with it.&quot;\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A manager asks for a firm date on a task with an unfamiliar technology. What is a good response?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Offer a range, name the assumptions and risks, propose a short spike to reduce uncertainty, and promise a firmer date after it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reduce uncertainty, then commit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: estimating and planning work fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Why estimates are wrong\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Give a range and state assumptions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Slice and de-risk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan for the unknown and say no\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Why estimates are wrong to Give a range and state assumptions; 2, Give a range and state assumptions to Slice and de-risk; 3, Slice and de-risk to Plan for the unknown and say no.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Why estimates are wrong \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> People estimate the happy path and forget reviews, testing, deployment, waiting for others, meetings, incidents and the discovery of unknowns.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Giving one number with no assumptions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Give a range and state assumptions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Say &quot;two to four weeks, most likely three, assuming the API owner reviews within two days and we reuse the existing auth module&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Estimating only coding time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Slice and de-risk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Break the work into slices that each deliver something testable, ideally in a few days.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving the riskiest part to last.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Plan for the unknown and say no \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keep slack: a plan fully booked at 100 percent fails at the first surprise.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Planning at 100 percent capacity.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-three-week-migration\">Incident: The three-week migration\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer estimated three weeks for moving a service to a new platform, and it took four months.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls jobs/ | wc -l\ngrep -l &quot;legacy-env&quot; jobs/* | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span>/ | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">214\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy-env&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span>/* | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">97\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The estimate assumed 20 simple jobs, but there were 214, and nearly half read environment variables from the old platform. No one had counted them. The lead restarted with an inventory, grouped jobs by pattern, migrated the easiest group first and gave updated ranges every two weeks. The final plan was honest but late. The next project began with a two-day inventory spike, and its estimate was within 15 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving one number with no assumptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Estimating only coding time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the riskiest part to last\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planning at 100 percent capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-estimates-are-wrong","text":"Why estimates are wrong"},{"id":"give-a-range-and-state-assumptions","text":"Give a range and state assumptions"},{"id":"slice-and-de-risk","text":"Slice and de-risk"},{"id":"plan-for-the-unknown-and-say-no-clearly","text":"Plan for the unknown and say no clearly"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-three-week-migration","text":"Incident: The three-week migration"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Atlassian: agile estimation","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/agile/project-management/estimation"},{"t":"Google SRE book: embracing risk","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/"},{"t":"DORA research","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"\"How long will it take?\" is the question every senior engineer must answer, and every estimate is partly a guess. The aim is not to be exact but to be useful: a range with stated assumptions, updated as you learn, so that others can plan around it. What you will learn Why estimates fail and how to reduce the error Giving ranges and confidence instead of single dates Slicing work and finding the risky parts first Planning for operations, review and the unknown Why estimates are wrong People estimate the happy path and forget reviews, testing, deployment, waiting for others, meetings, incidents and the discovery of unknowns. They anchor on the first number they heard, and they feel pressure to give a short one. Work with new technology or unclear requirements is the most uncertain. Past data helps more than intuition: compare with similar tasks you finished and how long they really took. Give a range and state assumptions Say \"two to four weeks, most likely three, assuming the API owner reviews within two days and we reuse the existing auth module\". A range tells the listener the uncertainty. A useful technique is three points: best case, likely and worst case. Convert the range into a commitment only when risk is reduced: \"I&#39;ll know more after the spike on Thursday, and then I&#39;ll give a date I&#39;d stake my name on.\" Tell people when an assumption breaks, as early as you notice. estimate: task: \"Migrate billing jobs to the new scheduler\" range_weeks: [ 3 , 6 ] most_likely_weeks: 4 confidence: medium assumptions: - \"Scheduler team supports the retry feature by week 2\" - \"No change to the invoice format\" risks: - \"Hidden dependency on cron environment variables\" first_step: \"Two-day spike to list all jobs and dependencies\" next_update: \"Friday\" Slice and de-risk Break the work into slices that each deliver something testable, ideally in a few days. Do the riskiest or most uncertain parts first (a spike or prototype), so bad news arrives early. Avoid \"90 percent done\": define done as running in production with monitoring and a documented rollback. Include time for code review, tests, documentation, migration and cleanup, and for being on call. For shared work, map dependencies on other teams and ask them for their own dates. Plan for the unknown and say no clearly Keep slack: a plan fully booked at 100 percent fails at the first surprise. A rough rule is to plan 60 to 70 percent of capacity for committed work, leaving the rest for incidents, support and learning. When asked to shorten a schedule, offer options instead of only refusing: reduce scope, add a phase, move the date, or add help (which has a startup cost). Make trade-offs visible: \"We can ship by the 15th without the audit log, or by the 29th with it.\" Predict first. A manager asks for a firm date on a task with an unfamiliar technology. What is a good response? Your guess Answer: Offer a range, name the assumptions and risks, propose a short spike to reduce uncertainty, and promise a firmer date after it. Reduce uncertainty, then commit. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: estimating and planning work fits together Flow in order: 1, Why estimates are wrong to Give a range and state assumptions; 2, Give a range and state assumptions to Slice and de-risk; 3, Slice and de-risk to Plan for the unknown and say no. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Why estimates are wrong Step What it does: People estimate the happy path and forget reviews, testing, deployment, waiting for others, meetings, incidents and the discovery of unknowns. If it fails or is weak: Giving one number with no assumptions. Give a range and state assumptions Data store What it does: Say \"two to four weeks, most likely three, assum"},"sre/senior-skills-running-incident-and-postmortem-meetings":{"html":"\u003cp>Technical skill finds the cause. Facilitation decides whether the group finds it quickly and learns from it afterward. Both the live incident call and the review meeting are skills you can practice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running an incident call: roles, cadence and decisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping the room focused and calm\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Facilitating a blameless post-mortem meeting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning discussion into actions that happen\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-incident-call\">The incident call\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Open with a single sentence of the problem and who is the \u003cstrong>incident commander\u003c/strong> (IC). The IC coordinates and decides; they do not debug. Assign a \u003cstrong>scribe\u003c/strong> to record a timeline, a \u003cstrong>communications lead\u003c/strong> to update stakeholders on a fixed cadence, and \u003cstrong>subject experts\u003c/strong> for each area. Use one channel for the call and one for the written record.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"incident_call:\n  opening: &quot;SEV2: checkout errors above 5 percent since 14:05. I am IC. Priya scribes. Omar on comms.&quot;\n  cadence:\n    internal_update_minutes: 15\n    customer_update_minutes: 30\n  rules:\n    - &quot;One person speaks at a time; the IC calls on experts.&quot;\n    - &quot;Say what you are about to do before you do it.&quot;\n    - &quot;Prefer mitigation (rollback, failover, scale) over finding root cause.&quot;\n    - &quot;Hand off the IC role explicitly after 60 to 90 minutes.&quot;\n  closing: &quot;State the mitigation, the next update time and who owns follow-up.&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">incident_call:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">opening:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SEV2: checkout errors above 5 percent since 14:05. I am IC. Priya scribes. Omar on comms.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cadence:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">internal_update_minutes:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">customer_update_minutes:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;One person speaks at a time; the IC calls on experts.&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Say what you are about to do before you do it.&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Prefer mitigation (rollback, failover, scale) over finding root cause.&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hand off the IC role explicitly after 60 to 90 minutes.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">closing:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;State the mitigation, the next update time and who owns follow-up.&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Repeat key facts (&quot;so we agree: rollback of release 482 is running, next check in five minutes&quot;), and keep side discussions out of the main call. Do not let theories become facts: label them as hypotheses and test the cheapest first. Call a break and hand over the role when people are tired.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"calm-and-clear\">Calm and clear\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tone is contagious. Use plain language, avoid blame, and keep updates factual: what we know, what we do not know, what we are doing, when the next update comes. If executives join, give them a separate update channel so they do not pull the technical call off course.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-post-mortem-meeting\">The post-mortem meeting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send the draft timeline before the meeting, so the time is spent on understanding, not on reading. The facilitator (not the person who made the change) sets ground rules: blameless, curious, focused on systems and decisions. Ask &quot;how did this make sense at the time?&quot; and &quot;what would have made it easier?&quot; instead of &quot;who caused it&quot;. Walk through the timeline, mark moments of detection, decision and recovery, and ask where information was missing. Capture contributing factors, not a single root cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"actions-that-happen\">Actions that happen\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Aim for a few, specific, owned actions, each with a date and a way to verify: &quot;Add an alert on queue age above 5 minutes, owner Omar, by 30 April&quot; is good; &quot;improve monitoring&quot; is not. Classify them as prevent, detect, mitigate or learn. Track them in the team&#39;s normal tracker and review completion at the next meeting. A post-mortem whose actions are not done is theatre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> During a call, three engineers argue about the cause while the error rate stays high. What should the IC do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Pause the debate, ask for the cheapest mitigation (such as rollback), assign one person per hypothesis to test, and set a short check-in time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Mitigate first, then investigate.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: running incident calls and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The incident call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Calm and clear\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The post-mortem meeting\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Actions that happen\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The incident call to Calm and clear; 2, Calm and clear to The post-mortem meeting; 3, The post-mortem meeting to Actions that happen.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The incident call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Open with a single sentence of the problem and who is the incident commander (IC).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating theories as facts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Calm and clear \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tone is contagious. Use plain language, avoid blame, and keep updates factual: what we know, what we do not know, what we are doing, when the next update comes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No clear IC, or an IC who also debugs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>The post-mortem meeting \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send the draft timeline before the meeting, so the time is spent on understanding, not on reading.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Actions that happen \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Aim for a few, specific, owned actions, each with a date and a way to verify: &quot;Add an alert on queue age above 5 minutes, owner Omar, by 30 April&quot; is good; &quot;improve...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Blaming people in the review.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-call-with-seven-voices\">Incident: The call with seven voices\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A major outage lasted 90 minutes, and the review found the fix was available at minute 20.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;I think&quot; incident-call-transcript.txt\ngrep -n &quot;rollback&quot; incident-call-transcript.txt | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;I think&quot;\u003c/span> incident-call-transcript.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rollback&quot;\u003c/span> incident-call-transcript.txt | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12: maybe rollback?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41: rollback might help\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">96: ok doing the rollback\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nobody was IC, several people investigated the same hypothesis, and the rollback suggestion was lost in the chatter. The company introduced the IC role, a written scribe timeline and a rule: if a recent change might be the cause, rolling it back is tried first unless there is a reason not to. The next outage with a bad release was fixed in 12 minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No clear IC, or an IC who also debugs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating theories as facts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blaming people in the review\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vague actions without owners or dates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-incident-call","text":"The incident call"},{"id":"calm-and-clear","text":"Calm and clear"},{"id":"the-post-mortem-meeting","text":"The post-mortem meeting"},{"id":"actions-that-happen","text":"Actions that happen"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-call-with-seven-voices","text":"Incident: The call with seven voices"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: managing incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: postmortem culture","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"PagerDuty incident response guide","u":"https://response.pagerduty.com/"}],"text":"Technical skill finds the cause. Facilitation decides whether the group finds it quickly and learns from it afterward. Both the live incident call and the review meeting are skills you can practice. What you will learn Running an incident call: roles, cadence and decisions Keeping the room focused and calm Facilitating a blameless post-mortem meeting Turning discussion into actions that happen The incident call Open with a single sentence of the problem and who is the incident commander (IC). The IC coordinates and decides; they do not debug. Assign a scribe to record a timeline, a communications lead to update stakeholders on a fixed cadence, and subject experts for each area. Use one channel for the call and one for the written record. incident_call: opening: \"SEV2: checkout errors above 5 percent since 14:05. I am IC. Priya scribes. Omar on comms.\" cadence: internal_update_minutes: 15 customer_update_minutes: 30 rules: - \"One person speaks at a time; the IC calls on experts.\" - \"Say what you are about to do before you do it.\" - \"Prefer mitigation (rollback, failover, scale) over finding root cause.\" - \"Hand off the IC role explicitly after 60 to 90 minutes.\" closing: \"State the mitigation, the next update time and who owns follow-up.\" Repeat key facts (\"so we agree: rollback of release 482 is running, next check in five minutes\"), and keep side discussions out of the main call. Do not let theories become facts: label them as hypotheses and test the cheapest first. Call a break and hand over the role when people are tired. Calm and clear Tone is contagious. Use plain language, avoid blame, and keep updates factual: what we know, what we do not know, what we are doing, when the next update comes. If executives join, give them a separate update channel so they do not pull the technical call off course. The post-mortem meeting Send the draft timeline before the meeting, so the time is spent on understanding, not on reading. The facilitator (not the person who made the change) sets ground rules: blameless, curious, focused on systems and decisions. Ask \"how did this make sense at the time?\" and \"what would have made it easier?\" instead of \"who caused it\". Walk through the timeline, mark moments of detection, decision and recovery, and ask where information was missing. Capture contributing factors, not a single root cause. Actions that happen Aim for a few, specific, owned actions, each with a date and a way to verify: \"Add an alert on queue age above 5 minutes, owner Omar, by 30 April\" is good; \"improve monitoring\" is not. Classify them as prevent, detect, mitigate or learn. Track them in the team&#39;s normal tracker and review completion at the next meeting. A post-mortem whose actions are not done is theatre. Predict first. During a call, three engineers argue about the cause while the error rate stays high. What should the IC do? Your guess Answer: Pause the debate, ask for the cheapest mitigation (such as rollback), assign one person per hypothesis to test, and set a short check-in time. Mitigate first, then investigate. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: running incident calls and... fits together Flow in order: 1, The incident call to Calm and clear; 2, Calm and clear to The post-mortem meeting; 3, The post-mortem meeting to Actions that happen. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The incident call Step What it does: Open with a single sentence of the problem and who is the incident commander (IC). If it fails or is weak: Treating theories as facts. Calm and clear Step What it does: Tone is contagious. Use plain language, avoid blame, and keep updates factual: what we know, what we do not know, what we are doing, when the next update comes. If it fails or is weak: "},"sre/senior-skills-mentoring-and-growing-engineers":{"html":"\u003cp>Your impact as a senior engineer is measured partly by how much stronger the people around you become. Mentoring is not giving answers faster; it is helping others build judgment so they need you less over time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting up a mentoring relationship with a clear goal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Teaching through questions, pairing and stretch work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving feedback that is specific and kind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviews and delegation as growth tools\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-goals\">Start with goals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In the first conversation ask what the person wants in the next six to twelve months: a skill, a role, more scope, a certification. Agree on how often to meet (30 minutes every week or two is plenty), what each of you will prepare, and how you will know it works. Mentoring is a relationship with a purpose, not a social chat, though trust matters. Different people need different support: new joiners need orientation, mid-level engineers need scope and feedback, senior engineers need sponsorship and strategic perspective.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"teach-with-questions-and-pairing\">Teach with questions and pairing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When someone brings a problem, resist solving it immediately. Ask: &quot;What have you tried? What do you expect to see? What would you check next?&quot; The goal is to model a debugging method, not to display your knowledge. Pair on tasks and let the mentee drive, with you narrating your reasoning when you do take over. Share your mistakes and how you recovered, since it shows that errors are normal and fixable. Point to documents and tools, then ask them to improve the document afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stretch-and-delegate\">Stretch and delegate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Growth happens just beyond comfort. Give a task that is slightly bigger than the last one, with clear outcomes, boundaries and checkpoints, and stay available. Delegation is more than handing off work: explain the why, agree on what &quot;good&quot; looks like, agree when to check in, and let them own decisions within the boundaries. If the result is different from what you would do but works, accept it. Take the blame for failures publicly and share the credit for successes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"feedback-that-works\">Feedback that works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make it timely, specific, about behavior and impact, and offer a path forward. Use a simple pattern: situation, behavior, impact, next step.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"feedback:\n  situation: &quot;In yesterday's incident call&quot;\n  behavior: &quot;You posted the rollback plan in the channel before asking the IC&quot;\n  impact: &quot;The IC lost track of who owned the step and two people ran it&quot;\n  next_step: &quot;Say the plan out loud to the IC first, then post it. Let's practice in the next game day.&quot;\n  also_good: &quot;Your timeline notes were the clearest I have seen.&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">feedback:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">situation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;In yesterday&#x27;s incident call&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">behavior:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;You posted the rollback plan in the channel before asking the IC&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">impact:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The IC lost track of who owned the step and two people ran it&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">next_step:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Say the plan out loud to the IC first, then post it. Let&#x27;s practice in the next game day.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">also_good:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Your timeline notes were the clearest I have seen.&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Give positive feedback with the same specificity, since people repeat what is named. Ask for feedback on yourself too.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reviews-that-teach\">Reviews that teach\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In code and design reviews, separate must-fix from suggestions and learning notes, explain the reason (&quot;this retry has no backoff, which can overload the service during an outage&quot;), and ask questions (&quot;what happens when the queue is full?&quot;). Link to material. Praise good decisions. A review that only lists defects teaches fear; one that explains principles teaches judgment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A junior asks how to fix a failing deployment. You know the answer in ten seconds. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Ask what they have checked and what the logs say, guide them to the next check, and let them find it. Give the answer only if time is critical, and explain how you got there.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Teach the method, not just the answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: mentoring and growing other... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start with goals\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Teach with questions and pairing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stretch and delegate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Feedback that works\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reviews that teach\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start with goals to Teach with questions and pairing; 2, Teach with questions and pairing to Stretch and delegate; 3, Stretch and delegate to Feedback that works; 4, Feedback that works to Reviews that teach.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Start with goals \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In the first conversation ask what the person wants in the next six to twelve months: a skill, a role, more scope, a certification.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague feedback (&quot;do better&quot;) or only negative feedback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Teach with questions and pairing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When someone brings a problem, resist solving it immediately.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Solving everything yourself.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Stretch and delegate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Growth happens just beyond comfort. Give a task that is slightly bigger than the last one, with clear outcomes, boundaries and checkpoints, and stay available.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Delegating a task without context or boundaries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Feedback that works \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Make it timely, specific, about behavior and impact, and offer a path forward.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reviews that only list defects.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Reviews that teach \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In code and design reviews, separate must-fix from suggestions and learning notes, explain the reason (&quot;this retry has no backoff, which can overload the service during...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-helpful-rescuer\">Incident: The helpful rescuer\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A senior engineer fixed every problem that a new teammate brought, and after a year the teammate still could not handle an on-call shift alone.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git shortlog -sn --since=&quot;1 year ago&quot; -- services/billing | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git shortlog -sn --since=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1 year ago&quot;\u003c/span> -- services/billing | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   412 senior.engineer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    38 new.teammate\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The senior engineer worked quickly and meant well, but the teammate never practiced. When the senior left on holiday, the team had a billing incident and nobody dared to act. In the follow-up they set up a plan: the teammate shadowed on-call, then led with the senior as backup, then took a shift alone with a written escalation path. Within three months the teammate handled incidents calmly, and the senior had time for larger design work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Solving everything yourself\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vague feedback (&quot;do better&quot;) or only negative feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Delegating a task without context or boundaries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviews that only list defects\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-goals","text":"Start with goals"},{"id":"teach-with-questions-and-pairing","text":"Teach with questions and pairing"},{"id":"stretch-and-delegate","text":"Stretch and delegate"},{"id":"feedback-that-works","text":"Feedback that works"},{"id":"reviews-that-teach","text":"Reviews that teach"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-helpful-rescuer","text":"Incident: The helpful rescuer"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE workbook: team lifecycles","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/team-lifecycles/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: accelerating SREs to on-call","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/accelerating-sre-on-call/"},{"t":"PagerDuty incident response training","u":"https://response.pagerduty.com/training/"}],"text":"Your impact as a senior engineer is measured partly by how much stronger the people around you become. Mentoring is not giving answers faster; it is helping others build judgment so they need you less over time. What you will learn Setting up a mentoring relationship with a clear goal Teaching through questions, pairing and stretch work Giving feedback that is specific and kind Reviews and delegation as growth tools Start with goals In the first conversation ask what the person wants in the next six to twelve months: a skill, a role, more scope, a certification. Agree on how often to meet (30 minutes every week or two is plenty), what each of you will prepare, and how you will know it works. Mentoring is a relationship with a purpose, not a social chat, though trust matters. Different people need different support: new joiners need orientation, mid-level engineers need scope and feedback, senior engineers need sponsorship and strategic perspective. Teach with questions and pairing When someone brings a problem, resist solving it immediately. Ask: \"What have you tried? What do you expect to see? What would you check next?\" The goal is to model a debugging method, not to display your knowledge. Pair on tasks and let the mentee drive, with you narrating your reasoning when you do take over. Share your mistakes and how you recovered, since it shows that errors are normal and fixable. Point to documents and tools, then ask them to improve the document afterwards. Stretch and delegate Growth happens just beyond comfort. Give a task that is slightly bigger than the last one, with clear outcomes, boundaries and checkpoints, and stay available. Delegation is more than handing off work: explain the why, agree on what \"good\" looks like, agree when to check in, and let them own decisions within the boundaries. If the result is different from what you would do but works, accept it. Take the blame for failures publicly and share the credit for successes. Feedback that works Make it timely, specific, about behavior and impact, and offer a path forward. Use a simple pattern: situation, behavior, impact, next step. feedback: situation: \"In yesterday&#x27;s incident call\" behavior: \"You posted the rollback plan in the channel before asking the IC\" impact: \"The IC lost track of who owned the step and two people ran it\" next_step: \"Say the plan out loud to the IC first, then post it. Let&#x27;s practice in the next game day.\" also_good: \"Your timeline notes were the clearest I have seen.\" Give positive feedback with the same specificity, since people repeat what is named. Ask for feedback on yourself too. Reviews that teach In code and design reviews, separate must-fix from suggestions and learning notes, explain the reason (\"this retry has no backoff, which can overload the service during an outage\"), and ask questions (\"what happens when the queue is full?\"). Link to material. Praise good decisions. A review that only lists defects teaches fear; one that explains principles teaches judgment. Predict first. A junior asks how to fix a failing deployment. You know the answer in ten seconds. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Ask what they have checked and what the logs say, guide them to the next check, and let them find it. Give the answer only if time is critical, and explain how you got there. Teach the method, not just the answer. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: mentoring and growing other... fits together Flow in order: 1, Start with goals to Teach with questions and pairing; 2, Teach with questions and pairing to Stretch and delegate; 3, Stretch and delegate to Feedback that works; 4, Feedback that works to Reviews that teach. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Start with goals Ste"},"sre/senior-skills-influencing-and-stakeholder-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Most important reliability and platform work crosses team boundaries, and you rarely control the people whose cooperation you need. Influence is the ability to move work forward through trust, clarity and shared goals rather than through rank.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Map stakeholders and what each one cares about\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build a case with evidence and options\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Negotiate and say no with alternatives\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep people informed, and handle conflict calmly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"map-the-people\">Map the people\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For any initiative, list who is affected, who decides, who can block and who can help. For each one, note what they want and what they fear: a product manager wants delivery dates, a security lead wants fewer exposed credentials, an on-call engineer wants fewer pages, finance wants predictable cost. Your proposal should answer each one&#39;s concern in their language. Talk to important stakeholders before any meeting, so nobody is surprised publicly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"stakeholders:\n  - name: &quot;Checkout team lead&quot;\n    role: &quot;can block&quot;\n    wants: &quot;No change freeze during the sale&quot;\n    worry: &quot;Migration risk before peak season&quot;\n    plan: &quot;Offer rollout after the sale and a canary on the staging flow&quot;\n  - name: &quot;Security&quot;\n    role: &quot;decides&quot;\n    wants: &quot;Short-lived credentials&quot;\n    worry: &quot;Exceptions that become permanent&quot;\n    plan: &quot;Show audit logs and expiry enforced by the platform&quot;\n  - name: &quot;Finance partner&quot;\n    role: &quot;informed&quot;\n    wants: &quot;Forecast within 10 percent&quot;\n    worry: &quot;Surprises in the bill&quot;\n    plan: &quot;Monthly cost note with unit costs&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stakeholders:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Checkout team lead&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;can block&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">wants:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;No change freeze during the sale&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">worry:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Migration risk before peak season&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">plan:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Offer rollout after the sale and a canary on the staging flow&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Security&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;decides&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">wants:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Short-lived credentials&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">worry:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Exceptions that become permanent&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">plan:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Show audit logs and expiry enforced by the platform&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Finance partner&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;informed&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">wants:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Forecast within 10 percent&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">worry:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Surprises in the bill&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">plan:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Monthly cost note with unit costs&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-the-case\">Build the case\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Lead with the problem and its cost to the listener: pages per week, hours lost, risk to revenue, audit findings. Use data from your own systems, one clear chart or number, not ten. Present options with trade-offs and your recommendation, and ask for a specific decision (&quot;I need approval for two engineers for one quarter&quot;). Start with a small pilot that proves value and lowers the risk of saying yes. Make the first step easy and reversible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"negotiate-and-decline-well\">Negotiate and decline well\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Understand the interest behind a demand: &quot;we need this by Friday&quot; may really mean &quot;we have a customer demo&quot;. Look for options that serve the interest at a lower cost. When you must say no, say it early, explain the constraint and offer an alternative: &quot;We cannot build the full tool this quarter. We can give you a script and a runbook now, and schedule the tool for next quarter.&quot; Be consistent: use the same criteria for everyone&#39;s requests, such as risk, user impact and effort, and show the queue so people see the trade-off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"no-surprises\">No surprises\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Share bad news early with your plan: &quot;The migration is two weeks late because of X. Here are the options.&quot; Send short, regular updates in a stable format (status, risks, decisions needed). Write down agreements and send a summary after meetings. In conflicts, separate the people from the problem, restate the other side&#39;s view fairly before disagreeing, and escalate only after trying direct conversation, and with the other party informed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A team refuses to adopt your new logging standard. What should you do before escalating?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Ask why. Learn their constraints, address them (tooling, migration help, a pilot), and show the benefit in their terms. Escalate only if that fails, and tell them first.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Understand the objection first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: influencing without authority and... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Map the people\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build the case\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Negotiate and decline well\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">No surprises\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Map the people to Build the case; 2, Build the case to Negotiate and decline well; 3, Negotiate and decline well to No surprises.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Map the people \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> For any initiative, list who is affected, who decides, who can block and who can help.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Surprising stakeholders with bad news late.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Build the case \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lead with the problem and its cost to the listener: pages per week, hours lost, risk to revenue, audit findings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Describing your needs, not theirs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Negotiate and decline well \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Understand the interest behind a demand: &quot;we need this by Friday&quot; may really mean &quot;we have a customer demo&quot;.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Springing proposals on people in meetings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>No surprises \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Share bad news early with your plan: &quot;The migration is two weeks late because of X.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-mandate-that-was-ignored\">Incident: The mandate that was ignored\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A platform team announced a mandatory new CI template by email, and six months later only three of forty teams had adopted it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -l &quot;legacy-ci-template&quot; teams/*/pipeline.yaml | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy-ci-template&quot;\u003c/span> teams/*/pipeline.yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">37\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The announcement described what the platform team wanted, not what teams gained, and ignored the teams&#39; real obstacles: special build steps and no time for migration. After interviews, the platform team added support for custom steps, offered migration help and a clear benefit (builds 40 percent faster in a pilot), and published a dashboard of adoption without shaming anyone. Within a quarter thirty teams had moved voluntarily.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Springing proposals on people in meetings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describing your needs, not theirs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Saying no without an alternative\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Surprising stakeholders with bad news late\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"map-the-people","text":"Map the people"},{"id":"build-the-case","text":"Build the case"},{"id":"negotiate-and-decline-well","text":"Negotiate and decline well"},{"id":"no-surprises","text":"No surprises"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-mandate-that-was-ignored","text":"Incident: The mandate that was ignored"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: communication and collaboration","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/communication-and-collaboration/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: organizational change management","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/organizational-change/"},{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"}],"text":"Most important reliability and platform work crosses team boundaries, and you rarely control the people whose cooperation you need. Influence is the ability to move work forward through trust, clarity and shared goals rather than through rank. What you will learn Map stakeholders and what each one cares about Build a case with evidence and options Negotiate and say no with alternatives Keep people informed, and handle conflict calmly Map the people For any initiative, list who is affected, who decides, who can block and who can help. For each one, note what they want and what they fear: a product manager wants delivery dates, a security lead wants fewer exposed credentials, an on-call engineer wants fewer pages, finance wants predictable cost. Your proposal should answer each one&#39;s concern in their language. Talk to important stakeholders before any meeting, so nobody is surprised publicly. stakeholders: - name: \"Checkout team lead\" role: \"can block\" wants: \"No change freeze during the sale\" worry: \"Migration risk before peak season\" plan: \"Offer rollout after the sale and a canary on the staging flow\" - name: \"Security\" role: \"decides\" wants: \"Short-lived credentials\" worry: \"Exceptions that become permanent\" plan: \"Show audit logs and expiry enforced by the platform\" - name: \"Finance partner\" role: \"informed\" wants: \"Forecast within 10 percent\" worry: \"Surprises in the bill\" plan: \"Monthly cost note with unit costs\" Build the case Lead with the problem and its cost to the listener: pages per week, hours lost, risk to revenue, audit findings. Use data from your own systems, one clear chart or number, not ten. Present options with trade-offs and your recommendation, and ask for a specific decision (\"I need approval for two engineers for one quarter\"). Start with a small pilot that proves value and lowers the risk of saying yes. Make the first step easy and reversible. Negotiate and decline well Understand the interest behind a demand: \"we need this by Friday\" may really mean \"we have a customer demo\". Look for options that serve the interest at a lower cost. When you must say no, say it early, explain the constraint and offer an alternative: \"We cannot build the full tool this quarter. We can give you a script and a runbook now, and schedule the tool for next quarter.\" Be consistent: use the same criteria for everyone&#39;s requests, such as risk, user impact and effort, and show the queue so people see the trade-off. No surprises Share bad news early with your plan: \"The migration is two weeks late because of X. Here are the options.\" Send short, regular updates in a stable format (status, risks, decisions needed). Write down agreements and send a summary after meetings. In conflicts, separate the people from the problem, restate the other side&#39;s view fairly before disagreeing, and escalate only after trying direct conversation, and with the other party informed. Predict first. A team refuses to adopt your new logging standard. What should you do before escalating? Your guess Answer: Ask why. Learn their constraints, address them (tooling, migration help, a pilot), and show the benefit in their terms. Escalate only if that fails, and tell them first. Understand the objection first. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: influencing without authority and... fits together Flow in order: 1, Map the people to Build the case; 2, Build the case to Negotiate and decline well; 3, Negotiate and decline well to No surprises. Step Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Map the people Step What it does: For any initiative, list who is affected, who decides, who can block and who can help. If it fails or is weak: Surprising stakeholders with bad news late. Build the case Step Wha"},"sre/senior-skills-interviewing-and-hiring-as-the-interviewer":{"html":"\u003cp>Hiring is one of the highest-leverage things a senior engineer does. A weak process rejects good people and admits poor fits, and costs the team for years. Structure, shared criteria and respect for the candidate make interviews both fairer and more accurate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define what the role needs before you ask a question\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design questions that reveal how a person works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Score against criteria and avoid common biases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the candidate experience professional\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-role\">Start with the role\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write what success looks like at six and twelve months, and turn it into three to five \u003cstrong>competencies\u003c/strong>: for example troubleshooting, system design, automation, communication and incident judgment, with expected depth for the level. Each interviewer owns different competencies, so the panel covers the role without repeating questions. Agree on what a strong, acceptable and weak answer looks like \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> the interviews.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"questions-that-show-how-someone-thinks\">Questions that show how someone thinks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prefer realistic scenarios over trivia: &quot;A service&#39;s latency doubled at 14:00. Walk me through what you do.&quot; Listen for method (scope the problem, form hypotheses, check the cheapest signals first, communicate), not for a single correct answer. Use follow-up probes to go deeper: &quot;What would you look at next if the CPU is fine?&quot; For design, give a small, concrete problem and let the candidate ask questions. For hands-on skill, a short realistic exercise (read a log, fix a failing script) beats whiteboard puzzles. Ask about past incidents: what they did, what they would change, what they learned. Accept different valid approaches.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"scorecard:\n  role: &quot;Platform engineer, L4&quot;\n  competencies:\n    troubleshooting:\n      strong: &quot;Forms hypotheses, tests cheaply, narrows quickly, explains reasoning&quot;\n      acceptable: &quot;Reaches the cause with some guidance&quot;\n      weak: &quot;Guesses randomly or changes many things at once&quot;\n    communication:\n      strong: &quot;Clear, checks understanding, states uncertainty&quot;\n      weak: &quot;Vague, defensive, or unable to explain choices&quot;\n  rule: &quot;Write notes during the interview; score within 24 hours; do not discuss before submitting&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scorecard:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Platform engineer, L4&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">competencies:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">troubleshooting:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strong:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Forms hypotheses, tests cheaply, narrows quickly, explains reasoning&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">acceptable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Reaches the cause with some guidance&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weak:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Guesses randomly or changes many things at once&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">communication:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strong:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Clear, checks understanding, states uncertainty&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weak:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Vague, defensive, or unable to explain choices&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rule:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Write notes during the interview; score within 24 hours; do not discuss before submitting&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"score-fairly\">Score fairly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Take notes of what was said and done, not impressions. Submit your score \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> talking to other interviewers, to avoid anchoring on the loudest opinion. Watch for biases: liking people who resemble you, favoring a famous employer, judging confidence over competence, and the &quot;halo&quot; from one great answer. Ask &quot;what evidence do I have?&quot; for each competency. Decide on the evidence against the criteria, and give a clear recommendation with reasons. Review outcomes later: do your scores predict performance?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-candidate-s-experience\">The candidate&#39;s experience\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Be on time, introduce yourself and the format, explain what you will do and leave time for their questions. Give hints when someone is stuck, since you are measuring ability, not the ability to cope with stress. Do not ask illegal or irrelevant personal questions. Give feedback quickly, and be kind and honest in rejections. Candidates who feel respected speak well of the company even when they do not join, and your process is the first view they get of how the team works.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Three interviewers share their impressions in a group chat before submitting scores. What is the risk?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Anchoring and groupthink: later opinions bend toward the first strong view. Submit independent written scores first, then discuss differences.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Independent evidence before discussion.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Senior skills: interviewing and hiring as the... fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start with the role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Questions that show how someone\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Score fairly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The candidate's experience\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start with the role to Questions that show how someone; 2, Questions that show how someone to Score fairly; 3, Score fairly to The candidate's experience.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Start with the role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write what success looks like at six and twelve months, and turn it into three to five competencies: for example troubleshooting, system design, automation...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Discussing before submitting scores.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Questions that show how someone \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prefer realistic scenarios over trivia: &quot;A service's latency doubled at 14:00.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Judging on trivia and gut feeling.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Score fairly \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Take notes of what was said and done, not impressions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Submit your score before talking to other interviewers, to avoid anchoring on the loudest opinion.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>The candidate's experience \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Be on time, introduce yourself and the format, explain what you will do and leave time for their questions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A poor candidate experience.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-brilliant-jerk-who-nearly-got-hired\">Incident: The brilliant jerk who nearly got hired\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A candidate impressed one interviewer so much with trivia that the panel overlooked repeated signs of poor collaboration in other rounds.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -i &quot;talked over\\|dismissive\\|would not explain&quot; interview-notes/*.txt | wc -l\ngrep -c &quot;strong hire&quot; interview-notes/*.txt\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;talked over\\|dismissive\\|would not explain&quot;\u003c/span> interview-notes/*.txt | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;strong hire&quot;\u003c/span> interview-notes/*.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Five separate interviewers had written concerns about collaboration, but the first interviewer&#39;s enthusiasm dominated the debrief. The hiring manager noticed the pattern in the notes and asked the panel to weigh evidence against the written criteria. The team passed on the candidate. Afterward they added collaboration as an explicit competency with its own scoring, and made the independent-scoring rule mandatory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Asking whatever comes to mind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Judging on trivia and gut feeling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Discussing before submitting scores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A poor candidate experience\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-role","text":"Start with the role"},{"id":"questions-that-show-how-someone-thinks","text":"Questions that show how someone thinks"},{"id":"score-fairly","text":"Score fairly"},{"id":"the-candidate-s-experience","text":"The candidate's experience"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-brilliant-jerk-who-nearly-got-hired","text":"Incident: The brilliant jerk who nearly got hired"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE workbook: team lifecycles","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/team-lifecycles/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: accelerating SREs to on-call","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/accelerating-sre-on-call/"},{"t":"Atlassian team playbook","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/team-playbook"}],"text":"Hiring is one of the highest-leverage things a senior engineer does. A weak process rejects good people and admits poor fits, and costs the team for years. Structure, shared criteria and respect for the candidate make interviews both fairer and more accurate. What you will learn Define what the role needs before you ask a question Design questions that reveal how a person works Score against criteria and avoid common biases Keep the candidate experience professional Start with the role Write what success looks like at six and twelve months, and turn it into three to five competencies : for example troubleshooting, system design, automation, communication and incident judgment, with expected depth for the level. Each interviewer owns different competencies, so the panel covers the role without repeating questions. Agree on what a strong, acceptable and weak answer looks like before the interviews. Questions that show how someone thinks Prefer realistic scenarios over trivia: \"A service&#39;s latency doubled at 14:00. Walk me through what you do.\" Listen for method (scope the problem, form hypotheses, check the cheapest signals first, communicate), not for a single correct answer. Use follow-up probes to go deeper: \"What would you look at next if the CPU is fine?\" For design, give a small, concrete problem and let the candidate ask questions. For hands-on skill, a short realistic exercise (read a log, fix a failing script) beats whiteboard puzzles. Ask about past incidents: what they did, what they would change, what they learned. Accept different valid approaches. scorecard: role: \"Platform engineer, L4\" competencies: troubleshooting: strong: \"Forms hypotheses, tests cheaply, narrows quickly, explains reasoning\" acceptable: \"Reaches the cause with some guidance\" weak: \"Guesses randomly or changes many things at once\" communication: strong: \"Clear, checks understanding, states uncertainty\" weak: \"Vague, defensive, or unable to explain choices\" rule: \"Write notes during the interview; score within 24 hours; do not discuss before submitting\" Score fairly Take notes of what was said and done, not impressions. Submit your score before talking to other interviewers, to avoid anchoring on the loudest opinion. Watch for biases: liking people who resemble you, favoring a famous employer, judging confidence over competence, and the \"halo\" from one great answer. Ask \"what evidence do I have?\" for each competency. Decide on the evidence against the criteria, and give a clear recommendation with reasons. Review outcomes later: do your scores predict performance? The candidate&#39;s experience Be on time, introduce yourself and the format, explain what you will do and leave time for their questions. Give hints when someone is stuck, since you are measuring ability, not the ability to cope with stress. Do not ask illegal or irrelevant personal questions. Give feedback quickly, and be kind and honest in rejections. Candidates who feel respected speak well of the company even when they do not join, and your process is the first view they get of how the team works. Predict first. Three interviewers share their impressions in a group chat before submitting scores. What is the risk? Your guess Answer: Anchoring and groupthink: later opinions bend toward the first strong view. Submit independent written scores first, then discuss differences. Independent evidence before discussion. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Senior skills: interviewing and hiring as the... fits together Flow in order: 1, Start with the role to Questions that show how someone; 2, Questions that show how someone to Score fairly; 3, Score fairly to The candidate's experience. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Start with the role Step"}});